{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RTVZT3Y3AiNBRuq3WyUHHV","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Planning Log: Hankweave Monorepo Migration\n     2→\n     3→## Areas Read\n     4→\n     5→- [x] source/hankweave/server - core runtime code (60+ .ts files, well-organized subdirs)\n     6→- [x] source/hankweave/package.json - @southbridgeai/hankweave v0.5.6, Bun runtime, many scripts\n     7→- [x] source/hankweave/scripts/ - build.ts, build-executable.ts, release.ts, sync-public.ts, generate-schemas.ts, e2e/init.ts, run-cross-runtime-tests.ts, test-sync-local.ts\n     8→- [x] source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ - ci.yml, release.yml, sync-public.yml\n     9→- [x] source/hankweave/.github/actions/ - setup-environment, run-tests, dump-test-logs (composite actions)\n    10→- [x] source/hankweave/biome.json - covers server/, tests/e2e/, tests/utils/, tests/unit/, types/\n    11→- [x] source/hankweave/bunfig.toml - minimal, just BUN_TEST env var for tests\n    12→- [x] source/hankweave/tsconfig.json - ES2022, strict, bun-types\n    13→- [x] source/hankweave/.gitignore, .npmignore, .gitattributes\n    14→- [x] source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md - comprehensive SOPs\n    15→- [x] source/hankweave-docs/ - Nextra 4, Next.js 15, Bun install+build\n    16→- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json - hankweave-docs v2.0.0\n    17→- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json - framework: nextjs, bun install/build\n    18→- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/tsconfig.json - ES2020, strict:false, bundler resolution\n    19→- [x] source/hankweave/tests/ - 65+ unit tests, 10+ integration, 10+ e2e, cross-runtime tests\n    20→- [x] plan/background/background.md - pre-compiled research\n    21→\n    22→## Aha Moments\n    23→\n    24→### 1. The docs project has a nested structure\n    25→The docs repo is `hankweave-docs/` at root but the actual Next.js project is at `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`. The `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `next.config.mjs` etc. are all inside `nextra-site/`. The docs repo root has `README.md`, `.gitignore`, `notes/`, `plan/`.\n    26→\n    27→This means that wherever docs lands in the monorepo, you need to decide: does the workspace point to the `nextra-site/` subdirectory (where package.json lives), or to the outer `hankweave-docs/` directory?\n    28→\n    29→**Decision**: The workspace should point to where `package.json` is — so the `nextra-site/` level. The outer `hankweave-docs/` scaffold (README, notes, plan) is development overhead that probably doesn't need its own workspace and can either be flattened or kept as-is.\n    30→\n    31→### 2. generate-schemas.ts is the only real cross-repo code reference\n    32→Grep confirms only `scripts/generate-schemas.ts:28` references `hankweave-docs` in actual code. All other references are in `intermediates/` planning documents (which are stripped on release). This simplifies the migration — only one file needs a path adjustment.\n    33→\n    34→### 3. The docs have NO dependency on the runtime package\n    35→The docs site (`hankweave-docs`) doesn't `import` anything from `@southbridgeai/hankweave`. The only cross-repo data flow is schemas generated by the runtime and written to the docs `public/schemas/` directory. A `workspace:*` dependency is NOT needed at this time.\n    36→\n    37→### 4. The public sync pipeline only needs to export the runtime\n    38→The `sync-public.ts` script works from the root of the hankweave repo. In a monorepo, if the runtime stays at the root, the sync script barely changes. If it moves into a subdirectory, the strip lists and snapshot process need reworking.\n    39→\n    40→### 5. CI uses composite actions with relative paths\n    41→All three composite actions (`.github/actions/*`) are referenced as `./.github/actions/setup-environment` etc. These use relative paths. As long as the `.github/` directory stays at the monorepo root, they work. The composite actions themselves just do `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and `bun test` — they'd work with workspaces too.\n    42→\n    43→### 6. The release script assumes it runs from the repo root\n    44→`release.ts` uses `resolve(process.cwd())` for paths and runs `git` commands. It also runs `bun run generate-schemas` and `bun run tc`. These all assume the cwd is the package root. In a monorepo with the runtime as a workspace, scripts would need to be run from the workspace directory or paths adjusted.\n    45→\n    46→### 7. build.ts uses relative paths from import.meta.dir\n    47→`build.ts` resolves `distDir` as `join(import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"dist\")` and entry point as `\"./server/index.ts\"`. This means build.ts must run from (or be relative to) the runtime package root. Same for `build-executable.ts`.\n    48→\n    49→### 8. Vercel monorepo support just needs \"Root Directory\"\n    50→Vercel lets you set the \"Root Directory\" for a project in project settings. The current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` would work if Vercel is pointed at the right subdirectory. This is a settings change, not a code change.\n    51→\n    52→### 9. The docs package.json has a `postbuild` step\n    53→`\"postbuild\": \"pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind\"` — this runs Pagefind after `next build`. This would work fine in a workspace since `next build` runs from the workspace directory.\n    54→\n    55→### 10. tsconfig.json settings differ significantly between the two projects\n    56→- Runtime: `target: ES2022`, `module: ES2022`, `moduleResolution: node`, `strict: true`, types: `bun-types`\n    57→- Docs: `target: ES2020`, `module: esnext`, `moduleResolution: bundler`, `strict: false`, plugins: `next`\n    58→\n    59→These configs should NOT be shared. Each workspace needs its own `tsconfig.json`.\n    60→\n    61→### 11. There's only one bun.lock file per project\n    62→Currently `source/hankweave/bun.lock` and `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/bun.lock`. In a monorepo, there would be a single root-level `bun.lock` file managed by Bun workspaces.\n    63→\n    64→### 12. The runtime's `.npmignore` strips `server/`, `scripts/`, `tests/` for npm publish\n    65→npm publish only includes `dist/`, `shims/`, `schemas/`. This is controlled by both `.npmignore` and the `\"files\"` field in `package.json`. In a monorepo, the npm publish command would need to run from the runtime workspace. `\"files\"` field should continue to work correctly.\n    66→\n    67→## Key Decision Points\n    68→\n    69→### Structure Decision: Runtime at root vs in a subdirectory\n    70→\n    71→**Option A: Runtime stays at repo root, docs moves in as a workspace**\n    72→```\n    73→hankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n    74→├── package.json            (root package.json with workspaces field, ALSO the runtime)\n    75→├── server/\n    76→├── scripts/\n    77→├── tests/\n    78→├── docs/                   (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n    79→│   ├── package.json\n    80→│   ├── app/\n    81→│   ├── content/\n    82→│   └── ...\n    83→└── ...\n    84→```\n    85→- Pro: Minimal disruption to existing paths, scripts, CI\n    86→- Pro: All existing relative paths in server/scripts still work\n    87→- Con: The root package.json serves dual purpose (workspace root AND runtime package)\n    88→- Con: `bun install` at root installs both workspace deps — might be slightly confusing\n    89→\n    90→**Option B: Both packages in subdirectories**\n    91→```\n    92→hankweave/                  (monorepo root)\n    93→├── package.json            (workspace root, not a package itself)\n    94→├── packages/\n    95→│   ├── runtime/            (was: hankweave root)\n    96→│   │   ├── package.json\n    97→│   │   ├── server/\n    98→│   │   └── ...\n    99→│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n   100→│       ├── package.json\n   101→│       └── ...\n   102→└── .github/\n   103→```\n   104→- Pro: Clean separation, conventional monorepo structure\n   105→- Con: MASSIVE amount of path changes — every script, CI workflow, build config, `import.meta.dir` reference\n   106→- Con: git history gets complicated\n   107→- Con: The release.ts, sync-public.ts, build.ts all need significant reworking\n   108→\n   109→**Option C: Runtime at root, docs as apps/docs**\n   110→```\n   111→hankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n   112→├── package.json            (root + runtime)\n   113→├── server/\n   114→├── apps/\n   115→│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n   116→│       ├── package.json\n   117→│       └── ...\n   118→└── ...\n   119→```\n   120→- Pro: Distinguishes \"apps\" (deployable) from packages (publishable) — common Turborepo pattern\n   121→- Con: `apps/` is a Turborepo convention that might be overengineering for two packages\n   122→- Otherwise similar to Option A\n   123→\n   124→### Recommendation: Option A (runtime at root)\n   125→\n   126→The path of least disruption. The runtime is the primary package — it has the most scripts, CI workflows, and tooling built around its position at root. Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing dozens of paths across scripts, workflows, and configs.\n   127→\n   128→The docs site is the smaller, simpler addition. Putting it at `docs/` (or `docs/nextra-site/`) within the runtime's repo structure is the minimal change.\n   129→\n   130→The trade-off: the root `package.json` serves double duty. But Bun workspaces handles this fine — you can have `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` in the root and the root itself is also a package.\n   131→\n   132→### Sync-public impact\n   133→\n   134→With Option A (runtime at root), the sync-public.ts barely changes. The only new item is that the `docs/` directory needs to be added to the `STRIP_DIRS` list so it doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot. Everything else stays the same.\n   135→\n   136→### CI impact\n   137→\n   138→With Option A:\n   139→- `ci.yml` barely changes — the runtime is still at root, `bun install` installs all workspaces, tests run from root\n   140→- `release.yml` same thing\n   141→- Optional: Add path filtering so docs-only changes skip runtime tests (nice to have, not required initially)\n   142→\n   143→### generate-schemas.ts impact\n   144→\n   145→The `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path changes from `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas` to `../docs/public/schemas` (one level up from `scripts/`, then into `docs/`). This is actually simpler.\n   146→\n   147→## Questions Resolved\n   148→\n   149→1. **Full directory structure** — mapped exhaustively\n   150→2. **Script invocation** — all via `bun run <script>` or `bun scripts/<file>.ts`\n   151→3. **Sync-public strips** — STRIP_DIRS + STRIP_FILES documented, understood\n   152→4. **Test infrastructure** — bun:test, 65+ unit, 10+ integration, e2e with mock LLMs\n   153→5. **server/ depth** — 3-4 levels (server/sentinels/, server/llm/, server/types/, etc.)\n   154→6. **Docs structure** — Nextra 4 + Next.js 15, content/ for MDX, app/ for routes, components/ for custom\n   155→\n   156→## Questions Remaining\n   157→\n   158→1. **Should the outer hankweave-docs/ wrapper be kept?** — The docs repo has a root-level README, notes/, plan/ directory outside nextra-site/. These are dev-only files. Probably flatten: just bring nextra-site/ contents into docs/.\n   159→2. **Shared dev dependencies?** — Both projects use TypeScript. The docs use `@types/node` and `@types/react`. The runtime uses `@types/bun`. Bun catalogs could share TypeScript version, but it's not critical.\n   160→3. **Does the monorepo need its own README at root?** — The current runtime README is internal. The public one is in `public-release-files/`. Adding a monorepo root README could be helpful but isn't strictly necessary for the migration.\n   161→\n   162→---\n   163→\n   164→## Codex Pass Notes (2026-03-05)\n   165→\n   166→### Immediate Questions / Unknowns\n   167→\n   168→- Vercel + Bun workspaces: confirm whether Vercel running `bun install` inside the docs root will correctly detect the workspace root and use the monorepo `bun.lock`, or whether we need explicit `installCommand`/`buildCommand` that `cd`s to the monorepo root first.\n   169→- Public sync transform: if the monorepo adds `\"workspaces\"` to the runtime `package.json`, decide whether `scripts/sync-public.ts` should delete `\"workspaces\"` when transforming `package.json` for the public snapshot.\n   170→- Docs content location mismatch: task spec references `nextra-site/pages/`, but this snapshot uses `nextra-site/content/` + `app/` (Nextra 4 + App Router). Any migration doc updates should reference `content/`, not `pages/`.\n   171→\n   172→### Codebase Conventions Noted (relevant to migration choices)\n   173→\n   174→- Runtime code is strict ESM TypeScript with explicit `.js` import specifiers (e.g., `server/index.ts` imports `./utils.js`).\n   175→- Runtime reads `package.json` via a path relative to `server/utils.ts` (`../package.json`), which implicitly assumes the runtime package layout stays stable.\n   176→- Build scripts (`scripts/build.ts`, `scripts/build-executable.ts`) assume they run with CWD = runtime root (`entrypoints: [\"./server/index.ts\"]` and lots of `import.meta.dir`-relative paths).\n   177→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"460b431c-bb40-4486-85aa-42380b742419","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/log.md","content":"# Planning Log: Hankweave Monorepo Migration\n\n## Areas Read\n\n- [x] source/hankweave/server - core runtime code (60+ .ts files, well-organized subdirs)\n- [x] source/hankweave/package.json - @southbridgeai/hankweave v0.5.6, Bun runtime, many scripts\n- [x] source/hankweave/scripts/ - build.ts, build-executable.ts, release.ts, sync-public.ts, generate-schemas.ts, e2e/init.ts, run-cross-runtime-tests.ts, test-sync-local.ts\n- [x] source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ - ci.yml, release.yml, sync-public.yml\n- [x] source/hankweave/.github/actions/ - setup-environment, run-tests, dump-test-logs (composite actions)\n- [x] source/hankweave/biome.json - covers server/, tests/e2e/, tests/utils/, tests/unit/, types/\n- [x] source/hankweave/bunfig.toml - minimal, just BUN_TEST env var for tests\n- [x] source/hankweave/tsconfig.json - ES2022, strict, bun-types\n- [x] source/hankweave/.gitignore, .npmignore, .gitattributes\n- [x] source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md - comprehensive SOPs\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/ - Nextra 4, Next.js 15, Bun install+build\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json - hankweave-docs v2.0.0\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json - framework: nextjs, bun install/build\n- [x] source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/tsconfig.json - ES2020, strict:false, bundler resolution\n- [x] source/hankweave/tests/ - 65+ unit tests, 10+ integration, 10+ e2e, cross-runtime tests\n- [x] plan/background/background.md - pre-compiled research\n\n## Aha Moments\n\n### 1. The docs project has a nested structure\nThe docs repo is `hankweave-docs/` at root but the actual Next.js project is at `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`. The `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `next.config.mjs` etc. are all inside `nextra-site/`. The docs repo root has `README.md`, `.gitignore`, `notes/`, `plan/`.\n\nThis means that wherever docs lands in the monorepo, you need to decide: does the workspace point to the `nextra-site/` subdirectory (where package.json lives), or to the outer `hankweave-docs/` directory?\n\n**Decision**: The workspace should point to where `package.json` is — so the `nextra-site/` level. The outer `hankweave-docs/` scaffold (README, notes, plan) is development overhead that probably doesn't need its own workspace and can either be flattened or kept as-is.\n\n### 2. generate-schemas.ts is the only real cross-repo code reference\nGrep confirms only `scripts/generate-schemas.ts:28` references `hankweave-docs` in actual code. All other references are in `intermediates/` planning documents (which are stripped on release). This simplifies the migration — only one file needs a path adjustment.\n\n### 3. The docs have NO dependency on the runtime package\nThe docs site (`hankweave-docs`) doesn't `import` anything from `@southbridgeai/hankweave`. The only cross-repo data flow is schemas generated by the runtime and written to the docs `public/schemas/` directory. A `workspace:*` dependency is NOT needed at this time.\n\n### 4. The public sync pipeline only needs to export the runtime\nThe `sync-public.ts` script works from the root of the hankweave repo. In a monorepo, if the runtime stays at the root, the sync script barely changes. If it moves into a subdirectory, the strip lists and snapshot process need reworking.\n\n### 5. CI uses composite actions with relative paths\nAll three composite actions (`.github/actions/*`) are referenced as `./.github/actions/setup-environment` etc. These use relative paths. As long as the `.github/` directory stays at the monorepo root, they work. The composite actions themselves just do `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and `bun test` — they'd work with workspaces too.\n\n### 6. The release script assumes it runs from the repo root\n`release.ts` uses `resolve(process.cwd())` for paths and runs `git` commands. It also runs `bun run generate-schemas` and `bun run tc`. These all assume the cwd is the package root. In a monorepo with the runtime as a workspace, scripts would need to be run from the workspace directory or paths adjusted.\n\n### 7. build.ts uses relative paths from import.meta.dir\n`build.ts` resolves `distDir` as `join(import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"dist\")` and entry point as `\"./server/index.ts\"`. This means build.ts must run from (or be relative to) the runtime package root. Same for `build-executable.ts`.\n\n### 8. Vercel monorepo support just needs \"Root Directory\"\nVercel lets you set the \"Root Directory\" for a project in project settings. The current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` would work if Vercel is pointed at the right subdirectory. This is a settings change, not a code change.\n\n### 9. The docs package.json has a `postbuild` step\n`\"postbuild\": \"pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind\"` — this runs Pagefind after `next build`. This would work fine in a workspace since `next build` runs from the workspace directory.\n\n### 10. tsconfig.json settings differ significantly between the two projects\n- Runtime: `target: ES2022`, `module: ES2022`, `moduleResolution: node`, `strict: true`, types: `bun-types`\n- Docs: `target: ES2020`, `module: esnext`, `moduleResolution: bundler`, `strict: false`, plugins: `next`\n\nThese configs should NOT be shared. Each workspace needs its own `tsconfig.json`.\n\n### 11. There's only one bun.lock file per project\nCurrently `source/hankweave/bun.lock` and `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/bun.lock`. In a monorepo, there would be a single root-level `bun.lock` file managed by Bun workspaces.\n\n### 12. The runtime's `.npmignore` strips `server/`, `scripts/`, `tests/` for npm publish\nnpm publish only includes `dist/`, `shims/`, `schemas/`. This is controlled by both `.npmignore` and the `\"files\"` field in `package.json`. In a monorepo, the npm publish command would need to run from the runtime workspace. `\"files\"` field should continue to work correctly.\n\n## Key Decision Points\n\n### Structure Decision: Runtime at root vs in a subdirectory\n\n**Option A: Runtime stays at repo root, docs moves in as a workspace**\n```\nhankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n├── package.json            (root package.json with workspaces field, ALSO the runtime)\n├── server/\n├── scripts/\n├── tests/\n├── docs/                   (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n│   ├── package.json\n│   ├── app/\n│   ├── content/\n│   └── ...\n└── ...\n```\n- Pro: Minimal disruption to existing paths, scripts, CI\n- Pro: All existing relative paths in server/scripts still work\n- Con: The root package.json serves dual purpose (workspace root AND runtime package)\n- Con: `bun install` at root installs both workspace deps — might be slightly confusing\n\n**Option B: Both packages in subdirectories**\n```\nhankweave/                  (monorepo root)\n├── package.json            (workspace root, not a package itself)\n├── packages/\n│   ├── runtime/            (was: hankweave root)\n│   │   ├── package.json\n│   │   ├── server/\n│   │   └── ...\n│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n│       ├── package.json\n│       └── ...\n└── .github/\n```\n- Pro: Clean separation, conventional monorepo structure\n- Con: MASSIVE amount of path changes — every script, CI workflow, build config, `import.meta.dir` reference\n- Con: git history gets complicated\n- Con: The release.ts, sync-public.ts, build.ts all need significant reworking\n\n**Option C: Runtime at root, docs as apps/docs**\n```\nhankweave/                  (monorepo root = runtime package root)\n├── package.json            (root + runtime)\n├── server/\n├── apps/\n│   └── docs/               (was: hankweave-docs/nextra-site)\n│       ├── package.json\n│       └── ...\n└── ...\n```\n- Pro: Distinguishes \"apps\" (deployable) from packages (publishable) — common Turborepo pattern\n- Con: `apps/` is a Turborepo convention that might be overengineering for two packages\n- Otherwise similar to Option A\n\n### Recommendation: Option A (runtime at root)\n\nThe path of least disruption. The runtime is the primary package — it has the most scripts, CI workflows, and tooling built around its position at root. Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing dozens of paths across scripts, workflows, and configs.\n\nThe docs site is the smaller, simpler addition. Putting it at `docs/` (or `docs/nextra-site/`) within the runtime's repo structure is the minimal change.\n\nThe trade-off: the root `package.json` serves double duty. But Bun workspaces handles this fine — you can have `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` in the root and the root itself is also a package.\n\n### Sync-public impact\n\nWith Option A (runtime at root), the sync-public.ts barely changes. The only new item is that the `docs/` directory needs to be added to the `STRIP_DIRS` list so it doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot. Everything else stays the same.\n\n### CI impact\n\nWith Option A:\n- `ci.yml` barely changes — the runtime is still at root, `bun install` installs all workspaces, tests run from root\n- `release.yml` same thing\n- Optional: Add path filtering so docs-only changes skip runtime tests (nice to have, not required initially)\n\n### generate-schemas.ts impact\n\nThe `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path changes from `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas` to `../docs/public/schemas` (one level up from `scripts/`, then into `docs/`). This is actually simpler.\n\n## Questions Resolved\n\n1. **Full directory structure** — mapped exhaustively\n2. **Script invocation** — all via `bun run <script>` or `bun scripts/<file>.ts`\n3. **Sync-public strips** — STRIP_DIRS + STRIP_FILES documented, understood\n4. **Test infrastructure** — bun:test, 65+ unit, 10+ integration, e2e with mock LLMs\n5. **server/ depth** — 3-4 levels (server/sentinels/, server/llm/, server/types/, etc.)\n6. **Docs structure** — Nextra 4 + Next.js 15, content/ for MDX, app/ for routes, components/ for custom\n\n## Questions Remaining\n\n1. **Should the outer hankweave-docs/ wrapper be kept?** — The docs repo has a root-level README, notes/, plan/ directory outside nextra-site/. These are dev-only files. Probably flatten: just bring nextra-site/ contents into docs/.\n2. **Shared dev dependencies?** — Both projects use TypeScript. The docs use `@types/node` and `@types/react`. The runtime uses `@types/bun`. Bun catalogs could share TypeScript version, but it's not critical.\n3. **Does the monorepo need its own README at root?** — The current runtime README is internal. The public one is in `public-release-files/`. Adding a monorepo root README could be helpful but isn't strictly necessary for the migration.\n\n---\n\n## Codex Pass Notes (2026-03-05)\n\n### Immediate Questions / Unknowns\n\n- Vercel + Bun workspaces: confirm whether Vercel running `bun install` inside the docs root will correctly detect the workspace root and use the monorepo `bun.lock`, or whether we need explicit `installCommand`/`buildCommand` that `cd`s to the monorepo root first.\n- Public sync transform: if the monorepo adds `\"workspaces\"` to the runtime `package.json`, decide whether `scripts/sync-public.ts` should delete `\"workspaces\"` when transforming `package.json` for the public snapshot.\n- Docs content location mismatch: task spec references `nextra-site/pages/`, but this snapshot uses `nextra-site/content/` + `app/` (Nextra 4 + App Router). Any migration doc updates should reference `content/`, not `pages/`.\n\n### Codebase Conventions Noted (relevant to migration choices)\n\n- Runtime code is strict ESM TypeScript with explicit `.js` import specifiers (e.g., `server/index.ts` imports `./utils.js`).\n- Runtime reads `package.json` via a path relative to `server/utils.ts` (`../package.json`), which implicitly assumes the runtime package layout stays stable.\n- Build scripts (`scripts/build.ts`, `scripts/build-executable.ts`) assume they run with CWD = runtime root (`entrypoints: [\"./server/index.ts\"]` and lots of `import.meta.dir`-relative paths).\n","numLines":177,"startLine":1,"totalLines":177}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:57.846Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GbJN4JZEbY5Z7Ax6b7iBE7","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Addendum: Documentation and Deployment Considerations\n     2→\n     3→*Read alongside `plan.md`. Covers documentation updates, Vercel deployment changes, and the docs workspace integration details.*\n     4→\n     5→---\n     6→\n     7→## 1. What the documentation materials reveal\n     8→\n     9→### Documentation site structure\n    10→\n    11→The docs site is a Nextra 4 + Next.js 15 application with this structure:\n    12→\n    13→```\n    14→nextra-site/                        (becomes docs/ in monorepo)\n    15→├── app/                            Next.js app directory\n    16→│   ├── layout.tsx                  Root layout with Navbar, Footer, PostHog\n    17→│   ├── posthog-provider.tsx        PostHog analytics wrapper\n    18→│   └── [[...mdxPath]]/page.tsx     Catch-all MDX route\n    19→├── content/                        MDX documentation pages\n    20→│   ├── _meta.ts                    Top-level navigation\n    21→│   ├── index.mdx                   Landing page\n    22→│   ├── concepts/                   Core Concepts (codons, hanks, rigs, loops, etc.)\n    23→│   ├── guides/                     Getting Started, Building, Testing, Debugging\n    24→│   ├── reference/                  CLI, Config, API Keys, Protocol, etc.\n    25→│   └── examples/                   Example hanks\n    26→├── components/                     Custom React components (MobileSearchFab, etc.)\n    27→├── public/                         Static assets\n    28→│   ├── images/                     Logos, diagrams, screenshots\n    29→│   └── schemas/                    JSON schemas (generated by runtime's generate-schemas.ts)\n    30→├── styles/                         Global CSS\n    31→├── next.config.mjs                 Nextra + Next.js config\n    32→├── package.json                    hankweave-docs v2.0.0\n    33→├── tsconfig.json                   ES2020, bundler moduleResolution\n    34→└── vercel.json                     Framework: nextjs, bun install/build\n    35→```\n    36→\n    37→### Key observations from docs content\n    38→\n    39→1. **No runtime imports**: The docs site doesn't import TypeScript modules from the runtime. It references hankweave as a CLI tool (`npx hankweave`, `bunx hankweave`) but doesn't use it as a library dependency.\n    40→\n    41→2. **Schema hosting**: The `public/schemas/` directory hosts JSON schemas generated by the runtime's `generate-schemas.ts`. This is the only data flow from runtime to docs. The schemas are served as static files — they're referenced from the docs pages via URLs like `/schemas/hank-file.schema.json`.\n    42→\n    43→3. **Layout references public repo**: `app/layout.tsx:64` links to `https://github.com/SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` as the project link. This doesn't change — the public mirror continues to be the user-facing repo.\n    44→\n    45→4. **PostHog analytics**: The docs site uses PostHog for analytics (`app/posthog-provider.tsx`). This is a client-side integration and doesn't affect the migration.\n    46→\n    47→5. **Pagefind search**: The `postbuild` script runs `pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind` to generate a search index. This runs relative to the docs workspace directory and will work in the monorepo.\n    48→\n    49→---\n    50→\n    51→## 2. Documentation that needs updating\n    52→\n    53→### 2.1: `standard-operating-procedures.md` — Repository Structure (Section 1)\n    54→\n    55→**File**: `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md:10-30`\n    56→\n    57→Update the directory tree to include `docs/`:\n    58→\n    59→```markdown\n    60→## 1. Repository Structure\n    61→\n    62→```\n    63→hankweave-dev/              ← private repo (SouthBridgeAI/hankweave)\n    64→├── server/                 ← runtime source code (TypeScript, Bun)\n    65→├── scripts/                ← build, release, sync, and dev tooling\n    66→├── tests/                  ← unit, integration, e2e, cross-runtime\n    67→├── schemas/                ← generated JSON schemas (from Zod)\n    68→├── shims/                  ← agent harness adapters\n    69→├── docs/                   ← documentation site workspace (Next.js + Nextra)\n    70→│   ├── app/                ← Next.js app directory\n    71→│   ├── content/            ← MDX documentation pages\n    72→│   ├── components/         ← custom React components\n    73→│   ├── public/             ← static assets + generated schemas\n    74→│   └── package.json        ← hankweave-docs workspace\n    75→├── learning/               ← example hanks and guides (ships to public)\n    76→├── intermediates/          ← planning docs (internal only)\n    77→├── external-docs/          ← SDK reference docs for agent context (internal only)\n    78→├── public-release-files/   ← files overlaid onto public repo during sync\n    79→├── assets/                 ← images, demo GIFs\n    80→├── .github/                ← CI workflows and composite actions\n    81→├── CHANGELOG.md            ← Keep a Changelog format\n    82→├── CONTRIBUTING.md         ← branch model and PR guidelines (internal only)\n    83→└── package.json            ← @southbridgeai/hankweave + workspace root\n    84→```\n    85→\n    86→There is a separate public repo at `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` which receives sanitized snapshots via the sync workflow. It publishes the public `hankweave` npm package.\n    87→\n    88→The documentation site (`docs/`) deploys to Vercel from this repo. It is a Bun workspace but does not have a workspace dependency on the runtime package.\n    89→```\n    90→\n    91→### 2.2: `standard-operating-procedures.md` — Strip List (Section 1)\n    92→\n    93→**File**: `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md:34-36`\n    94→\n    95→Add `docs/` to the \"What gets stripped\" list:\n    96→\n    97→```markdown\n    98→### What gets stripped before public release\n    99→\n   100→The sync process (`scripts/sync-public.ts`) removes internal-only content:\n   101→\n   102→- **Directories**: `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `.github/actions/`, `docs/`\n   103→```\n   104→\n   105→### 2.3: `standard-operating-procedures.md` — Key Files Reference (Section 9)\n   106→\n   107→**File**: `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md:337-354`\n   108→\n   109→Add docs workspace entry:\n   110→\n   111→```markdown\n   112→| `docs/package.json`                    | Documentation site workspace (hankweave-docs)                                 |\n   113→| `docs/content/`                        | MDX documentation pages (concepts, guides, reference)                         |\n   114→```\n   115→\n   116→### 2.4: `standard-operating-procedures.md` — Development Workflow (Section 3)\n   117→\n   118→Consider adding a note about the docs workspace:\n   119→\n   120→```markdown\n   121→### Documentation site\n   122→\n   123→The documentation site is a Bun workspace at `docs/`. To build it locally:\n   124→\n   125→```bash\n   126→cd docs && bun run dev    # Development server (hot reload)\n   127→cd docs && bun run build  # Production build\n   128→```\n   129→\n   130→Changes to docs pages (`docs/content/**/*.mdx`) are deployed automatically via Vercel when pushed to the configured branch.\n   131→\n   132→When generating schemas, they are automatically written to both `schemas/` (for the npm package) and `docs/public/schemas/` (for the docs site):\n   133→\n   134→```bash\n   135→bun run generate-schemas\n   136→```\n   137→```\n   138→\n   139→---\n   140→\n   141→## 3. Documentation that should NOT be updated\n   142→\n   143→### User-facing docs (`docs/content/**/*.mdx`)\n   144→\n   145→The user-facing documentation covers:\n   146→- **Concepts**: Codons, Hanks, Rigs, Loops, Checkpoints, Sentinels, Execution Flow\n   147→- **Guides**: Getting Started, Building Hanks, Testing, Debugging, Docker\n   148→- **Reference**: CLI, Configuration, API Keys, Protocol, Event Journal\n   149→\n   150→None of these reference the repository structure. They document Hankweave as a product/CLI tool. The monorepo migration is an internal change that doesn't affect users.\n   151→\n   152→### Public README (`public-release-files/README.md`)\n   153→\n   154→This file is overlaid onto the public mirror (`hankweave-runtime`). It describes installation, usage, and links to documentation. The monorepo structure is invisible to public users.\n   155→\n   156→### `CHANGELOG.md`\n   157→\n   158→The changelog documents user-facing changes. The monorepo migration is an infrastructure change. It could be mentioned under an \"Internal\" or \"Changed\" section if desired, but it's not user-facing:\n   159→\n   160→```markdown\n   161→## [Unreleased]\n   162→\n   163→### Changed\n   164→\n   165→- **Monorepo migration** — Consolidated documentation site into the development repository as a Bun workspace. No changes to runtime behavior or public-facing interfaces.\n   166→```\n   167→\n   168→This is optional.\n   169→\n   170→---\n   171→\n   172→## 4. Vercel deployment details\n   173→\n   174→### Current state\n   175→\n   176→The docs site currently deploys from `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs`. Vercel is configured to:\n   177→1. Connect to the `hankweave-docs` GitHub repo\n   178→2. Build with `bun run build` (as specified in `vercel.json`)\n   179→3. Use Next.js framework detection\n   180→4. Deploy to the configured domain\n   181→\n   182→### Required changes\n   183→\n   184→**Step 1: Connect Vercel to the monorepo**\n   185→\n   186→In the Vercel dashboard:\n   187→1. Go to the hankweave-docs project settings\n   188→2. Under \"Git\" → \"Connected Git Repository\"\n   189→3. Change from `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`\n   190→4. Set \"Root Directory\" to `docs`\n   191→\n   192→**Step 2: Configure Ignored Build Step (optional but recommended)**\n   193→\n   194→To prevent unnecessary docs rebuilds when only runtime code changes:\n   195→1. Go to Settings → General → \"Ignored Build Step\"\n   196→2. Set to: `git diff --quiet HEAD^ HEAD -- docs/`\n   197→\n   198→This tells Vercel to skip the build if no files under `docs/` have changed.\n   199→\n   200→**Step 3: Verify `vercel.json` is at the right level**\n   201→\n   202→The `vercel.json` currently in `nextra-site/` (which becomes `docs/`) contains:\n   203→```json\n   204→{\n   205→  \"framework\": \"nextjs\",\n   206→  \"installCommand\": \"bun install\",\n   207→  \"buildCommand\": \"bun run build\"\n   208→}\n   209→```\n   210→\n   211→When Vercel's root directory is set to `docs`, it will look for `vercel.json` in `docs/vercel.json`. This is correct.\n   212→\n   213→**Important**: The `installCommand: \"bun install\"` in `vercel.json` runs from `docs/`, but because it's part of a Bun workspace, Bun will install the root lockfile and all workspace dependencies. This should work, but verify:\n   214→- Vercel may need the root `package.json` accessible to resolve the workspace configuration\n   215→- If Vercel only mounts the `docs/` subdirectory, workspace resolution might fail\n   216→\n   217→**Fallback**: If workspace resolution fails in Vercel, change the root directory to `/` (repo root) and update `buildCommand` to `cd docs && bun run build`. The `installCommand` stays as `bun install` (at root).\n   218→\n   219→**Step 4: Verify branch configuration**\n   220→\n   221→Vercel should deploy from the branch where docs changes land. Currently this is likely `main` or `master` of `hankweave-docs`. In the monorepo, the branches are `develop` and `release/alpha`. Configure Vercel to:\n   222→- Production deployment: `release/alpha` (or `develop`, depending on preference)\n   223→- Preview deployments: PRs targeting `develop`\n   224→\n   225→### Things that don't change\n   226→\n   227→- The `next.config.mjs` stays the same\n   228→- The `postbuild` pagefind step stays the same\n   229→- The PostHog integration stays the same\n   230→- The domain/DNS stays the same\n   231→\n   232→---\n   233→\n   234→## 5. Cross-repo schema flow after migration\n   235→\n   236→### Before migration\n   237→\n   238→```\n   239→hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts\n   240→  → writes to hankweave/schemas/                    (local, always works)\n   241→  → writes to ../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas/  (sibling repo, works only if checked out)\n   242→```\n   243→\n   244→### After migration\n   245→\n   246→```\n   247→hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts\n   248→  → writes to hankweave/schemas/                    (local, always works)\n   249→  → writes to hankweave/docs/public/schemas/         (same repo, always works)\n   250→```\n   251→\n   252→The `docsAvailable` check in `generate-schemas.ts` becomes permanently true (the `docs/` directory is always present in the monorepo). The check should be kept for safety but will never evaluate to false in normal operation.\n   253→\n   254→### Release flow for schemas\n   255→\n   256→1. Developer runs `bun run generate-schemas` (or it runs automatically during release)\n   257→2. Schemas are written to both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`\n   258→3. Both locations are committed together\n   259→4. When the runtime is synced to public, `schemas/` goes with it\n   260→5. When docs deploy to Vercel, `docs/public/schemas/` goes with it\n   261→6. Both consumers always have the latest schemas — no coordination needed\n   262→\n   263→This is the primary benefit of the monorepo: what was previously a fragile cross-repo file write is now a simple intra-repo operation that's always consistent.\n   264→\n   265→---\n   266→\n   267→## 6. Implications and risks from docs review\n   268→\n   269→### 6.1: `hankweave-docs` repo archival\n   270→\n   271→After the monorepo migration, the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` repo becomes orphaned. It should be:\n   272→1. Archived (read-only) on GitHub\n   273→2. README updated to say \"This repo has been merged into SouthBridgeAI/hankweave\"\n   274→3. Vercel disconnected (after the new Vercel connection is verified)\n   275→\n   276→### 6.2: The `notes/` and `plan/` directories\n   277→\n   278→The docs repo has `hankweave-docs/notes/` and `hankweave-docs/plan/` directories outside `nextra-site/`. These are internal planning documents. Options:\n   279→- **Discard**: They're historical and won't be needed going forward\n   280→- **Archive**: Copy them into `intermediates/` as a numbered entry (e.g., `intermediates/docs-migration/legacy-notes/`)\n   281→- **Include in docs/**: Keep them at `docs/notes/` and `docs/plan/` (they won't be built by Next.js since they're not in `content/` or `app/`)\n   282→\n   283→Recommendation: Discard. If the team wants them preserved, archive in `intermediates/`.\n   284→\n   285→### 6.3: Build environment variable\n   286→\n   287→The `vercel.json` contains:\n   288→```json\n   289→{\n   290→  \"build\": {\n   291→    \"env\": {\n   292→      \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\"\n   293→    }\n   294→  }\n   295→}\n   296→```\n   297→\n   298→This environment variable is specific to the docs Vercel build and will continue to work in the same `vercel.json` at `docs/vercel.json`. No change needed, but worth noting for awareness.\n   299→\n   300→### 6.4: No new docs pages needed\n   301→\n   302→The monorepo migration is purely internal infrastructure. No new user-facing documentation pages are needed. The guides, concepts, and reference pages all document Hankweave's behavior and interface, not its repository layout.\n   303→\n   304→---\n   305→\n   306→## 7. Assumptions revisited after docs review\n   307→\n   308→### Assumption: \"The docs have no dependency on the runtime package\"\n   309→\n   310→**Confirmed.** After reviewing the full docs source (layout.tsx, page components, MDX content), there are zero imports from `@southbridgeai/hankweave` or any runtime module. The only data flow is the schema JSON files generated by the runtime's script.\n   311→\n   312→### Assumption: \"Vercel can build the docs from a monorepo subdirectory\"\n   313→\n   314→**Mostly confirmed, with a caveat.** Vercel supports monorepos with root directory configuration. However, Bun workspaces require the root `package.json` to be accessible for workspace resolution. If Vercel only mounts the subdirectory, `bun install` in `docs/` might not find the root `package.json` and might treat `docs/` as a standalone project (which would still work — just without workspace hoisting).\n   315→\n   316→**Mitigation**: If this is an issue, set Vercel root directory to `/` and update the build command to `cd docs && bun run build`.\n   317→\n   318→### Assumption: \"No shared development dependencies worth consolidating\"\n   319→\n   320→**Confirmed.** The only shared dependency is `typescript`. The runtime uses Bun-specific APIs and `bun-types`, while the docs use React, Next.js, and Nextra. There's no benefit to Bun catalogs or shared configs at this point.\n   321→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"fbd670d1-9e45-426a-b289-ff1804d53734","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/addendum-docs.md","content":"# Addendum: Documentation and Deployment Considerations\n\n*Read alongside `plan.md`. Covers documentation updates, Vercel deployment changes, and the docs workspace integration details.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the documentation materials reveal\n\n### Documentation site structure\n\nThe docs site is a Nextra 4 + Next.js 15 application with this structure:\n\n```\nnextra-site/                        (becomes docs/ in monorepo)\n├── app/                            Next.js app directory\n│   ├── layout.tsx                  Root layout with Navbar, Footer, PostHog\n│   ├── posthog-provider.tsx        PostHog analytics wrapper\n│   └── [[...mdxPath]]/page.tsx     Catch-all MDX route\n├── content/                        MDX documentation pages\n│   ├── _meta.ts                    Top-level navigation\n│   ├── index.mdx                   Landing page\n│   ├── concepts/                   Core Concepts (codons, hanks, rigs, loops, etc.)\n│   ├── guides/                     Getting Started, Building, Testing, Debugging\n│   ├── reference/                  CLI, Config, API Keys, Protocol, etc.\n│   └── examples/                   Example hanks\n├── components/                     Custom React components (MobileSearchFab, etc.)\n├── public/                         Static assets\n│   ├── images/                     Logos, diagrams, screenshots\n│   └── schemas/                    JSON schemas (generated by runtime's generate-schemas.ts)\n├── styles/                         Global CSS\n├── next.config.mjs                 Nextra + Next.js config\n├── package.json                    hankweave-docs v2.0.0\n├── tsconfig.json                   ES2020, bundler moduleResolution\n└── vercel.json                     Framework: nextjs, bun install/build\n```\n\n### Key observations from docs content\n\n1. **No runtime imports**: The docs site doesn't import TypeScript modules from the runtime. It references hankweave as a CLI tool (`npx hankweave`, `bunx hankweave`) but doesn't use it as a library dependency.\n\n2. **Schema hosting**: The `public/schemas/` directory hosts JSON schemas generated by the runtime's `generate-schemas.ts`. This is the only data flow from runtime to docs. The schemas are served as static files — they're referenced from the docs pages via URLs like `/schemas/hank-file.schema.json`.\n\n3. **Layout references public repo**: `app/layout.tsx:64` links to `https://github.com/SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` as the project link. This doesn't change — the public mirror continues to be the user-facing repo.\n\n4. **PostHog analytics**: The docs site uses PostHog for analytics (`app/posthog-provider.tsx`). This is a client-side integration and doesn't affect the migration.\n\n5. **Pagefind search**: The `postbuild` script runs `pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind` to generate a search index. This runs relative to the docs workspace directory and will work in the monorepo.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Documentation that needs updating\n\n### 2.1: `standard-operating-procedures.md` — Repository Structure (Section 1)\n\n**File**: `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md:10-30`\n\nUpdate the directory tree to include `docs/`:\n\n```markdown\n## 1. Repository Structure\n\n```\nhankweave-dev/              ← private repo (SouthBridgeAI/hankweave)\n├── server/                 ← runtime source code (TypeScript, Bun)\n├── scripts/                ← build, release, sync, and dev tooling\n├── tests/                  ← unit, integration, e2e, cross-runtime\n├── schemas/                ← generated JSON schemas (from Zod)\n├── shims/                  ← agent harness adapters\n├── docs/                   ← documentation site workspace (Next.js + Nextra)\n│   ├── app/                ← Next.js app directory\n│   ├── content/            ← MDX documentation pages\n│   ├── components/         ← custom React components\n│   ├── public/             ← static assets + generated schemas\n│   └── package.json        ← hankweave-docs workspace\n├── learning/               ← example hanks and guides (ships to public)\n├── intermediates/          ← planning docs (internal only)\n├── external-docs/          ← SDK reference docs for agent context (internal only)\n├── public-release-files/   ← files overlaid onto public repo during sync\n├── assets/                 ← images, demo GIFs\n├── .github/                ← CI workflows and composite actions\n├── CHANGELOG.md            ← Keep a Changelog format\n├── CONTRIBUTING.md         ← branch model and PR guidelines (internal only)\n└── package.json            ← @southbridgeai/hankweave + workspace root\n```\n\nThere is a separate public repo at `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` which receives sanitized snapshots via the sync workflow. It publishes the public `hankweave` npm package.\n\nThe documentation site (`docs/`) deploys to Vercel from this repo. It is a Bun workspace but does not have a workspace dependency on the runtime package.\n```\n\n### 2.2: `standard-operating-procedures.md` — Strip List (Section 1)\n\n**File**: `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md:34-36`\n\nAdd `docs/` to the \"What gets stripped\" list:\n\n```markdown\n### What gets stripped before public release\n\nThe sync process (`scripts/sync-public.ts`) removes internal-only content:\n\n- **Directories**: `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `.github/actions/`, `docs/`\n```\n\n### 2.3: `standard-operating-procedures.md` — Key Files Reference (Section 9)\n\n**File**: `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md:337-354`\n\nAdd docs workspace entry:\n\n```markdown\n| `docs/package.json`                    | Documentation site workspace (hankweave-docs)                                 |\n| `docs/content/`                        | MDX documentation pages (concepts, guides, reference)                         |\n```\n\n### 2.4: `standard-operating-procedures.md` — Development Workflow (Section 3)\n\nConsider adding a note about the docs workspace:\n\n```markdown\n### Documentation site\n\nThe documentation site is a Bun workspace at `docs/`. To build it locally:\n\n```bash\ncd docs && bun run dev    # Development server (hot reload)\ncd docs && bun run build  # Production build\n```\n\nChanges to docs pages (`docs/content/**/*.mdx`) are deployed automatically via Vercel when pushed to the configured branch.\n\nWhen generating schemas, they are automatically written to both `schemas/` (for the npm package) and `docs/public/schemas/` (for the docs site):\n\n```bash\nbun run generate-schemas\n```\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Documentation that should NOT be updated\n\n### User-facing docs (`docs/content/**/*.mdx`)\n\nThe user-facing documentation covers:\n- **Concepts**: Codons, Hanks, Rigs, Loops, Checkpoints, Sentinels, Execution Flow\n- **Guides**: Getting Started, Building Hanks, Testing, Debugging, Docker\n- **Reference**: CLI, Configuration, API Keys, Protocol, Event Journal\n\nNone of these reference the repository structure. They document Hankweave as a product/CLI tool. The monorepo migration is an internal change that doesn't affect users.\n\n### Public README (`public-release-files/README.md`)\n\nThis file is overlaid onto the public mirror (`hankweave-runtime`). It describes installation, usage, and links to documentation. The monorepo structure is invisible to public users.\n\n### `CHANGELOG.md`\n\nThe changelog documents user-facing changes. The monorepo migration is an infrastructure change. It could be mentioned under an \"Internal\" or \"Changed\" section if desired, but it's not user-facing:\n\n```markdown\n## [Unreleased]\n\n### Changed\n\n- **Monorepo migration** — Consolidated documentation site into the development repository as a Bun workspace. No changes to runtime behavior or public-facing interfaces.\n```\n\nThis is optional.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Vercel deployment details\n\n### Current state\n\nThe docs site currently deploys from `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs`. Vercel is configured to:\n1. Connect to the `hankweave-docs` GitHub repo\n2. Build with `bun run build` (as specified in `vercel.json`)\n3. Use Next.js framework detection\n4. Deploy to the configured domain\n\n### Required changes\n\n**Step 1: Connect Vercel to the monorepo**\n\nIn the Vercel dashboard:\n1. Go to the hankweave-docs project settings\n2. Under \"Git\" → \"Connected Git Repository\"\n3. Change from `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`\n4. Set \"Root Directory\" to `docs`\n\n**Step 2: Configure Ignored Build Step (optional but recommended)**\n\nTo prevent unnecessary docs rebuilds when only runtime code changes:\n1. Go to Settings → General → \"Ignored Build Step\"\n2. Set to: `git diff --quiet HEAD^ HEAD -- docs/`\n\nThis tells Vercel to skip the build if no files under `docs/` have changed.\n\n**Step 3: Verify `vercel.json` is at the right level**\n\nThe `vercel.json` currently in `nextra-site/` (which becomes `docs/`) contains:\n```json\n{\n  \"framework\": \"nextjs\",\n  \"installCommand\": \"bun install\",\n  \"buildCommand\": \"bun run build\"\n}\n```\n\nWhen Vercel's root directory is set to `docs`, it will look for `vercel.json` in `docs/vercel.json`. This is correct.\n\n**Important**: The `installCommand: \"bun install\"` in `vercel.json` runs from `docs/`, but because it's part of a Bun workspace, Bun will install the root lockfile and all workspace dependencies. This should work, but verify:\n- Vercel may need the root `package.json` accessible to resolve the workspace configuration\n- If Vercel only mounts the `docs/` subdirectory, workspace resolution might fail\n\n**Fallback**: If workspace resolution fails in Vercel, change the root directory to `/` (repo root) and update `buildCommand` to `cd docs && bun run build`. The `installCommand` stays as `bun install` (at root).\n\n**Step 4: Verify branch configuration**\n\nVercel should deploy from the branch where docs changes land. Currently this is likely `main` or `master` of `hankweave-docs`. In the monorepo, the branches are `develop` and `release/alpha`. Configure Vercel to:\n- Production deployment: `release/alpha` (or `develop`, depending on preference)\n- Preview deployments: PRs targeting `develop`\n\n### Things that don't change\n\n- The `next.config.mjs` stays the same\n- The `postbuild` pagefind step stays the same\n- The PostHog integration stays the same\n- The domain/DNS stays the same\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cross-repo schema flow after migration\n\n### Before migration\n\n```\nhankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts\n  → writes to hankweave/schemas/                    (local, always works)\n  → writes to ../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas/  (sibling repo, works only if checked out)\n```\n\n### After migration\n\n```\nhankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts\n  → writes to hankweave/schemas/                    (local, always works)\n  → writes to hankweave/docs/public/schemas/         (same repo, always works)\n```\n\nThe `docsAvailable` check in `generate-schemas.ts` becomes permanently true (the `docs/` directory is always present in the monorepo). The check should be kept for safety but will never evaluate to false in normal operation.\n\n### Release flow for schemas\n\n1. Developer runs `bun run generate-schemas` (or it runs automatically during release)\n2. Schemas are written to both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`\n3. Both locations are committed together\n4. When the runtime is synced to public, `schemas/` goes with it\n5. When docs deploy to Vercel, `docs/public/schemas/` goes with it\n6. Both consumers always have the latest schemas — no coordination needed\n\nThis is the primary benefit of the monorepo: what was previously a fragile cross-repo file write is now a simple intra-repo operation that's always consistent.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Implications and risks from docs review\n\n### 6.1: `hankweave-docs` repo archival\n\nAfter the monorepo migration, the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` repo becomes orphaned. It should be:\n1. Archived (read-only) on GitHub\n2. README updated to say \"This repo has been merged into SouthBridgeAI/hankweave\"\n3. Vercel disconnected (after the new Vercel connection is verified)\n\n### 6.2: The `notes/` and `plan/` directories\n\nThe docs repo has `hankweave-docs/notes/` and `hankweave-docs/plan/` directories outside `nextra-site/`. These are internal planning documents. Options:\n- **Discard**: They're historical and won't be needed going forward\n- **Archive**: Copy them into `intermediates/` as a numbered entry (e.g., `intermediates/docs-migration/legacy-notes/`)\n- **Include in docs/**: Keep them at `docs/notes/` and `docs/plan/` (they won't be built by Next.js since they're not in `content/` or `app/`)\n\nRecommendation: Discard. If the team wants them preserved, archive in `intermediates/`.\n\n### 6.3: Build environment variable\n\nThe `vercel.json` contains:\n```json\n{\n  \"build\": {\n    \"env\": {\n      \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis environment variable is specific to the docs Vercel build and will continue to work in the same `vercel.json` at `docs/vercel.json`. No change needed, but worth noting for awareness.\n\n### 6.4: No new docs pages needed\n\nThe monorepo migration is purely internal infrastructure. No new user-facing documentation pages are needed. The guides, concepts, and reference pages all document Hankweave's behavior and interface, not its repository layout.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Assumptions revisited after docs review\n\n### Assumption: \"The docs have no dependency on the runtime package\"\n\n**Confirmed.** After reviewing the full docs source (layout.tsx, page components, MDX content), there are zero imports from `@southbridgeai/hankweave` or any runtime module. The only data flow is the schema JSON files generated by the runtime's script.\n\n### Assumption: \"Vercel can build the docs from a monorepo subdirectory\"\n\n**Mostly confirmed, with a caveat.** Vercel supports monorepos with root directory configuration. However, Bun workspaces require the root `package.json` to be accessible for workspace resolution. If Vercel only mounts the subdirectory, `bun install` in `docs/` might not find the root `package.json` and might treat `docs/` as a standalone project (which would still work — just without workspace hoisting).\n\n**Mitigation**: If this is an issue, set Vercel root directory to `/` and update the build command to `cd docs && bun run build`.\n\n### Assumption: \"No shared development dependencies worth consolidating\"\n\n**Confirmed.** The only shared dependency is `typescript`. The runtime uses Bun-specific APIs and `bun-types`, while the docs use React, Next.js, and Nextra. There's no benefit to Bun catalogs or shared configs at this point.\n","numLines":321,"startLine":1,"totalLines":321}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:57.862Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FxqmGeg5ZngbFGuHb3uZwf","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Addendum: Testing Considerations\n     2→\n     3→*Read alongside `plan.md`. Covers test impact, test patterns, and what to verify after the monorepo migration.*\n     4→\n     5→---\n     6→\n     7→## 1. What the supplementary materials reveal\n     8→\n     9→### Test infrastructure overview\n    10→\n    11→The test suite is organized in four tiers:\n    12→\n    13→| Tier | Location | Runner | Count | External deps |\n    14→|------|----------|--------|-------|--------------|\n    15→| Unit | `tests/unit/` | `bun:test` | 65+ files | None |\n    16→| Integration | `tests/integration/` | `bun:test` | 10+ files | Mock LLM |\n    17→| E2E | `tests/e2e/` | `bun:test` | 10+ files | Real API keys |\n    18→| Cross-runtime | `tests/cross-runtime/` | `bun`/`node`/`deno` | 3+ files | None |\n    19→\n    20→All tests import from `../../server/<module>` using relative paths from within the `tests/` directory. Since the `server/` directory is not moving, **no test imports need to change**.\n    21→\n    22→### Test runner configuration\n    23→\n    24→- `bunfig.toml` sets `BUN_TEST=1` environment variable for test runs\n    25→- `package.json` defines test scripts: `test:unit`, `test:integration`, `test`, `test:e2e:init`, `test:cross-runtime:*`\n    26→- CI uses composite action `.github/actions/run-tests/action.yml` which calls `bun test tests/unit` and `bun test tests/integration`\n    27→\n    28→### Test utilities\n    29→\n    30→Key test utilities at `tests/utils/`:\n    31→- `sentinel-test-harness.ts` — mock LLM calls, event tracking, test workspace management\n    32→- `mock-llm.ts` — typed mock LLM provider with configurable responses\n    33→- `env-test-helpers.ts` — environment variable capture/restore for test isolation\n    34→\n    35→None of these reference paths outside the runtime package.\n    36→\n    37→---\n    38→\n    39→## 2. Existing tests affected by the planned changes\n    40→\n    41→### Directly affected: None\n    42→\n    43→No existing test file needs modification. The migration changes:\n    44→1. Root `package.json` (adds `workspaces` field)\n    45→2. Scripts in `scripts/` (path adjustments)\n    46→3. `.gitignore` (docs artifacts)\n    47→4. SOP document\n    48→\n    49→None of these are read by tests. The `tests/unit/json-schema.test.ts` reads from `schemas/` which is unchanged. The `tests/unit/config.test.ts` reads from `server/config.ts` which is unchanged.\n    50→\n    51→### Potentially affected (low risk)\n    52→\n    53→1. **`tests/unit/json-schema.test.ts`** — Tests that generated schemas validate correctly. The schemas themselves don't change, only where they're also copied to (now `docs/public/schemas/` instead of `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas/`). The test reads from `schemas/` which is the runtime's local copy.\n    54→\n    55→2. **`tests/integration/schema-zod-parity.test.ts`** — Verifies Zod schemas match JSON schemas. Same as above — reads from `schemas/`, not affected.\n    56→\n    57→3. **Any test reading `package.json`** — The `\"workspaces\"` field addition shouldn't affect tests that read version or package name. Verified: `tests/unit/utils.test.ts` reads `getMetadata()` which extracts `name` and `version` from `package.json` — the `workspaces` field won't interfere.\n    58→\n    59→### Not affected: everything else\n    60→\n    61→All unit tests, integration tests, E2E tests, and cross-runtime tests operate on runtime code and mock data. They have no awareness of the docs workspace.\n    62→\n    63→---\n    64→\n    65→## 3. New tests to write\n    66→\n    67→### No new automated tests are strictly required\n    68→\n    69→The migration is a structural change (directory layout, workspace configuration) rather than a code change. The runtime behavior is identical before and after migration.\n    70→\n    71→### Recommended manual verification checklist\n    72→\n    73→After migration, verify by hand (or in a CI validation step):\n    74→\n    75→```bash\n    76→# 1. Workspace installs correctly\n    77→bun install\n    78→# Expected: success, single bun.lock at root, docs/node_modules linked\n    79→\n    80→# 2. Runtime tests still pass\n    81→bun run test:unit\n    82→bun run test:integration\n    83→bun run lint\n    84→bun run tc\n    85→\n    86→# 3. Schema generation writes to both locations\n    87→bun run generate-schemas\n    88→ls schemas/hank-file.schema.json\n    89→ls docs/public/schemas/hank-file.schema.json\n    90→\n    91→# 4. Runtime build works\n    92→bun run build\n    93→ls dist/index.js\n    94→\n    95→# 5. Docs build works\n    96→cd docs && bun run build && cd ..\n    97→\n    98→# 6. npm pack excludes docs\n    99→npm pack --dry-run\n   100→# Expected: docs/ NOT in the file list\n   101→\n   102→# 7. Sync dry-run strips docs correctly (requires git history)\n   103→bun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version <version> --use-head\n   104→# Expected: \"Correctly removed: docs\" in verification output\n   105→```\n   106→\n   107→### Optional: Add a workspace smoke test\n   108→\n   109→If desired, a lightweight test could be added to verify the monorepo structure is consistent:\n   110→\n   111→```typescript\n   112→// tests/unit/monorepo-structure.test.ts\n   113→import { describe, test, expect } from \"bun:test\";\n   114→import * as fs from \"node:fs\";\n   115→import * as path from \"node:path\";\n   116→\n   117→describe(\"monorepo structure\", () => {\n   118→  const ROOT = path.resolve(import.meta.dir, \"../..\");\n   119→\n   120→  test(\"root package.json declares docs workspace\", () => {\n   121→    const pkg = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(ROOT, \"package.json\"), \"utf8\"));\n   122→    expect(pkg.workspaces).toContain(\"docs\");\n   123→  });\n   124→\n   125→  test(\"docs workspace has package.json\", () => {\n   126→    expect(fs.existsSync(path.join(ROOT, \"docs\", \"package.json\"))).toBe(true);\n   127→  });\n   128→\n   129→  test(\"docs schemas directory exists\", () => {\n   130→    expect(fs.existsSync(path.join(ROOT, \"docs\", \"public\", \"schemas\"))).toBe(true);\n   131→  });\n   132→});\n   133→```\n   134→\n   135→This is lightweight and validates the structural invariant. Optional — only add if the team wants structural tests.\n   136→\n   137→---\n   138→\n   139→## 4. Edge cases to cover\n   140→\n   141→### 4.1: `bun install --frozen-lockfile` in CI\n   142→\n   143→After migration, the CI composite action (`.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`) runs:\n   144→```bash\n   145→bun install --frozen-lockfile\n   146→```\n   147→\n   148→This will fail if the `bun.lock` at root doesn't include the docs workspace's dependencies. **The `bun.lock` must be regenerated and committed as part of the migration.**\n   149→\n   150→### 4.2: The docs workspace's `devDependencies` in the root lockfile\n   151→\n   152→The docs workspace has these dependencies that the runtime doesn't:\n   153→- `next` ^15.0.0\n   154→- `nextra` ^4.0.0\n   155→- `nextra-theme-docs` ^4.0.0\n   156→- `react` ^19.0.0\n   157→- `react-dom` ^19.0.0\n   158→- `@vercel/analytics` ^1.6.1\n   159→- `posthog-js` ^1.347.2\n   160→- `pagefind` ^1.3.0\n   161→\n   162→These will be added to the root `bun.lock`. They should NOT conflict with the runtime's dependencies. The runtime doesn't use React or Next.js. The only shared dependency is `typescript` — both packages use it but can have independent versions.\n   163→\n   164→### 4.3: `npm publish` from root\n   165→\n   166→The `release.yml` runs `npm publish` from the repo root. The `\"files\"` field in `package.json`:\n   167→```json\n   168→\"files\": [\"dist\", \"shims\", \"schemas\"]\n   169→```\n   170→\n   171→This is an allowlist — `docs/` won't be included. Verify with `npm pack --dry-run` that the published tarball doesn't include docs workspace files.\n   172→\n   173→### 4.4: Build executable with workspace\n   174→\n   175→`build-executable.ts` uses `ENTRY_POINT = \"server/index.ts\"` and builds from root. Bun's build should resolve the entry point the same way with or without workspaces. Verify: `bun scripts/build-executable.ts` produces a working binary.\n   176→\n   177→### 4.5: Cross-runtime tests\n   178→\n   179→`scripts/run-cross-runtime-tests.ts` resolves `testsDir` from `import.meta.dirname`. This path doesn't change. Cross-runtime tests import from `../../server/` which doesn't change. Should work without modification.\n   180→\n   181→---\n   182→\n   183→## 5. Test patterns and conventions to follow\n   184→\n   185→If adding any new tests (e.g., the optional monorepo structure test):\n   186→\n   187→1. **Location**: `tests/unit/` for unit tests, `tests/integration/` for integration\n   188→2. **Import style**: `import { ... } from \"bun:test\"` for test framework, `import * as fs from \"node:fs\"` for Node APIs\n   189→3. **Relative imports**: `../../server/<module>` from test files\n   190→4. **Cleanup**: Create temp directories, clean up in `afterEach`/`afterAll`\n   191→5. **No global state**: Each test should be independent\n   192→6. **Biome compliance**: Tests in `tests/unit/` and `tests/e2e/` are covered by Biome linting\n   193→\n   194→---\n   195→\n   196→## 6. Conflicts or inconsistencies\n   197→\n   198→**None identified.** The plan and test infrastructure are consistent. The migration doesn't touch any runtime code, so all existing tests should pass without modification.\n   199→\n   200→The only \"inconsistency\" worth noting: the `generate-schemas.ts` script currently silently skips writing to docs if the path doesn't exist (`docsAvailable` check). After migration, this path will always exist, so the `docsAvailable` check becomes redundant. The plan recommends keeping the check (defensive) rather than removing it (premature cleanup).\n   201→\n   202→---\n   203→\n   204→## 7. Risks\n   205→\n   206→1. **Dependency hoisting conflicts**: Bun workspaces hoists dependencies. If the runtime and docs have conflicting versions of a shared transitive dependency, the hoist could cause issues. This is unlikely — the two packages have almost entirely different dependency trees (server-side Bun tools vs. React/Next.js).\n   207→\n   208→2. **CI time increase**: CI now installs docs dependencies even when only runtime code changed. This adds maybe 10-20 seconds to `bun install`. Not worth optimizing initially. Path filtering in CI can be added later.\n   209→\n   210→3. **Lockfile churn**: The unified `bun.lock` will see more frequent changes because it covers both workspaces. Docs dependency updates will modify the lockfile even when no runtime code is affected. This is normal for monorepos and a minor inconvenience.\n   211→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"c8afcf0a-da57-4ec7-86b2-f9428f89cd1c","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/addendum-tests.md","content":"# Addendum: Testing Considerations\n\n*Read alongside `plan.md`. Covers test impact, test patterns, and what to verify after the monorepo migration.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the supplementary materials reveal\n\n### Test infrastructure overview\n\nThe test suite is organized in four tiers:\n\n| Tier | Location | Runner | Count | External deps |\n|------|----------|--------|-------|--------------|\n| Unit | `tests/unit/` | `bun:test` | 65+ files | None |\n| Integration | `tests/integration/` | `bun:test` | 10+ files | Mock LLM |\n| E2E | `tests/e2e/` | `bun:test` | 10+ files | Real API keys |\n| Cross-runtime | `tests/cross-runtime/` | `bun`/`node`/`deno` | 3+ files | None |\n\nAll tests import from `../../server/<module>` using relative paths from within the `tests/` directory. Since the `server/` directory is not moving, **no test imports need to change**.\n\n### Test runner configuration\n\n- `bunfig.toml` sets `BUN_TEST=1` environment variable for test runs\n- `package.json` defines test scripts: `test:unit`, `test:integration`, `test`, `test:e2e:init`, `test:cross-runtime:*`\n- CI uses composite action `.github/actions/run-tests/action.yml` which calls `bun test tests/unit` and `bun test tests/integration`\n\n### Test utilities\n\nKey test utilities at `tests/utils/`:\n- `sentinel-test-harness.ts` — mock LLM calls, event tracking, test workspace management\n- `mock-llm.ts` — typed mock LLM provider with configurable responses\n- `env-test-helpers.ts` — environment variable capture/restore for test isolation\n\nNone of these reference paths outside the runtime package.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Existing tests affected by the planned changes\n\n### Directly affected: None\n\nNo existing test file needs modification. The migration changes:\n1. Root `package.json` (adds `workspaces` field)\n2. Scripts in `scripts/` (path adjustments)\n3. `.gitignore` (docs artifacts)\n4. SOP document\n\nNone of these are read by tests. The `tests/unit/json-schema.test.ts` reads from `schemas/` which is unchanged. The `tests/unit/config.test.ts` reads from `server/config.ts` which is unchanged.\n\n### Potentially affected (low risk)\n\n1. **`tests/unit/json-schema.test.ts`** — Tests that generated schemas validate correctly. The schemas themselves don't change, only where they're also copied to (now `docs/public/schemas/` instead of `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas/`). The test reads from `schemas/` which is the runtime's local copy.\n\n2. **`tests/integration/schema-zod-parity.test.ts`** — Verifies Zod schemas match JSON schemas. Same as above — reads from `schemas/`, not affected.\n\n3. **Any test reading `package.json`** — The `\"workspaces\"` field addition shouldn't affect tests that read version or package name. Verified: `tests/unit/utils.test.ts` reads `getMetadata()` which extracts `name` and `version` from `package.json` — the `workspaces` field won't interfere.\n\n### Not affected: everything else\n\nAll unit tests, integration tests, E2E tests, and cross-runtime tests operate on runtime code and mock data. They have no awareness of the docs workspace.\n\n---\n\n## 3. New tests to write\n\n### No new automated tests are strictly required\n\nThe migration is a structural change (directory layout, workspace configuration) rather than a code change. The runtime behavior is identical before and after migration.\n\n### Recommended manual verification checklist\n\nAfter migration, verify by hand (or in a CI validation step):\n\n```bash\n# 1. Workspace installs correctly\nbun install\n# Expected: success, single bun.lock at root, docs/node_modules linked\n\n# 2. Runtime tests still pass\nbun run test:unit\nbun run test:integration\nbun run lint\nbun run tc\n\n# 3. Schema generation writes to both locations\nbun run generate-schemas\nls schemas/hank-file.schema.json\nls docs/public/schemas/hank-file.schema.json\n\n# 4. Runtime build works\nbun run build\nls dist/index.js\n\n# 5. Docs build works\ncd docs && bun run build && cd ..\n\n# 6. npm pack excludes docs\nnpm pack --dry-run\n# Expected: docs/ NOT in the file list\n\n# 7. Sync dry-run strips docs correctly (requires git history)\nbun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version <version> --use-head\n# Expected: \"Correctly removed: docs\" in verification output\n```\n\n### Optional: Add a workspace smoke test\n\nIf desired, a lightweight test could be added to verify the monorepo structure is consistent:\n\n```typescript\n// tests/unit/monorepo-structure.test.ts\nimport { describe, test, expect } from \"bun:test\";\nimport * as fs from \"node:fs\";\nimport * as path from \"node:path\";\n\ndescribe(\"monorepo structure\", () => {\n  const ROOT = path.resolve(import.meta.dir, \"../..\");\n\n  test(\"root package.json declares docs workspace\", () => {\n    const pkg = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(ROOT, \"package.json\"), \"utf8\"));\n    expect(pkg.workspaces).toContain(\"docs\");\n  });\n\n  test(\"docs workspace has package.json\", () => {\n    expect(fs.existsSync(path.join(ROOT, \"docs\", \"package.json\"))).toBe(true);\n  });\n\n  test(\"docs schemas directory exists\", () => {\n    expect(fs.existsSync(path.join(ROOT, \"docs\", \"public\", \"schemas\"))).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n```\n\nThis is lightweight and validates the structural invariant. Optional — only add if the team wants structural tests.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Edge cases to cover\n\n### 4.1: `bun install --frozen-lockfile` in CI\n\nAfter migration, the CI composite action (`.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`) runs:\n```bash\nbun install --frozen-lockfile\n```\n\nThis will fail if the `bun.lock` at root doesn't include the docs workspace's dependencies. **The `bun.lock` must be regenerated and committed as part of the migration.**\n\n### 4.2: The docs workspace's `devDependencies` in the root lockfile\n\nThe docs workspace has these dependencies that the runtime doesn't:\n- `next` ^15.0.0\n- `nextra` ^4.0.0\n- `nextra-theme-docs` ^4.0.0\n- `react` ^19.0.0\n- `react-dom` ^19.0.0\n- `@vercel/analytics` ^1.6.1\n- `posthog-js` ^1.347.2\n- `pagefind` ^1.3.0\n\nThese will be added to the root `bun.lock`. They should NOT conflict with the runtime's dependencies. The runtime doesn't use React or Next.js. The only shared dependency is `typescript` — both packages use it but can have independent versions.\n\n### 4.3: `npm publish` from root\n\nThe `release.yml` runs `npm publish` from the repo root. The `\"files\"` field in `package.json`:\n```json\n\"files\": [\"dist\", \"shims\", \"schemas\"]\n```\n\nThis is an allowlist — `docs/` won't be included. Verify with `npm pack --dry-run` that the published tarball doesn't include docs workspace files.\n\n### 4.4: Build executable with workspace\n\n`build-executable.ts` uses `ENTRY_POINT = \"server/index.ts\"` and builds from root. Bun's build should resolve the entry point the same way with or without workspaces. Verify: `bun scripts/build-executable.ts` produces a working binary.\n\n### 4.5: Cross-runtime tests\n\n`scripts/run-cross-runtime-tests.ts` resolves `testsDir` from `import.meta.dirname`. This path doesn't change. Cross-runtime tests import from `../../server/` which doesn't change. Should work without modification.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Test patterns and conventions to follow\n\nIf adding any new tests (e.g., the optional monorepo structure test):\n\n1. **Location**: `tests/unit/` for unit tests, `tests/integration/` for integration\n2. **Import style**: `import { ... } from \"bun:test\"` for test framework, `import * as fs from \"node:fs\"` for Node APIs\n3. **Relative imports**: `../../server/<module>` from test files\n4. **Cleanup**: Create temp directories, clean up in `afterEach`/`afterAll`\n5. **No global state**: Each test should be independent\n6. **Biome compliance**: Tests in `tests/unit/` and `tests/e2e/` are covered by Biome linting\n\n---\n\n## 6. Conflicts or inconsistencies\n\n**None identified.** The plan and test infrastructure are consistent. The migration doesn't touch any runtime code, so all existing tests should pass without modification.\n\nThe only \"inconsistency\" worth noting: the `generate-schemas.ts` script currently silently skips writing to docs if the path doesn't exist (`docsAvailable` check). After migration, this path will always exist, so the `docsAvailable` check becomes redundant. The plan recommends keeping the check (defensive) rather than removing it (premature cleanup).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Risks\n\n1. **Dependency hoisting conflicts**: Bun workspaces hoists dependencies. If the runtime and docs have conflicting versions of a shared transitive dependency, the hoist could cause issues. This is unlikely — the two packages have almost entirely different dependency trees (server-side Bun tools vs. React/Next.js).\n\n2. **CI time increase**: CI now installs docs dependencies even when only runtime code changed. This adds maybe 10-20 seconds to `bun install`. Not worth optimizing initially. Path filtering in CI can be added later.\n\n3. **Lockfile churn**: The unified `bun.lock` will see more frequent changes because it covers both workspaces. Docs dependency updates will modify the lockfile even when no runtime code is affected. This is normal for monorepos and a minor inconvenience.\n","numLines":211,"startLine":1,"totalLines":211}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:57.876Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwebLtZ2nkNvTdXzXnpBqT","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Addendum 1: Tests + CI/CD Implications\n     2→\n     3→This addendum is meant to be read alongside `plan/rough-workspace/codex/plan.md`.\n     4→\n     5→## A) Thinking (what changed after reading tests)\n     6→\n     7→### A.1 What supplementary materials did I review?\n     8→\n     9→- Runtime test suite under `source/hankweave/tests/` (unit/integration/e2e/cross-runtime scaffolding).\n    10→- Runtime CI composite actions and workflows under `source/hankweave/.github/`.\n    11→\n    12→### A.2 What do these materials add that the main plan didn’t have?\n    13→\n    14→- CI runs tests from repo root via composite actions and assumes `bun install --frozen-lockfile` succeeds at the repo root. `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`.\n    15→- CI’s test action runs explicit runtime-only directories (`tests/unit`, `tests/integration`) and cross-runtime tests via scripts, so docs co-location should not directly expand the test scope unless we choose to add a docs build step. `source/hankweave/.github/actions/run-tests/action.yml:9`.\n    16→\n    17→### A.3 What existing tests are affected by the planned changes?\n    18→\n    19→- Expected: **no test code changes**, if runtime remains at repo root and docs is simply added as a subdirectory workspace.\n    20→- Indirectly affected: anything that depends on `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and the shape of `bun.lock` after workspace convergence.\n    21→\n    22→### A.4 What new tests (or CI checks) should be added?\n    23→\n    24→Recommended minimal additions:\n    25→\n    26→1. **Docs build check (CI job)**\n    27→   - Add a `docs-build` job that runs `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun run build` to prevent monorepo PRs from breaking docs silently.\n    28→\n    29→2. **Schema generation regression check (optional)**\n    30→   - A lightweight check that `bun run generate-schemas` succeeds in the monorepo and updates both:\n    31→     - runtime `schemas/`\n    32→     - docs `public/schemas/`\n    33→   - If schemas are committed, follow with `git diff --exit-code` to ensure the repo stays in sync.\n    34→\n    35→### A.5 Edge cases to cover in validation\n    36→\n    37→- `bun install --frozen-lockfile` from the monorepo root after merging lockfiles (this is what CI runs).\n    38→- Running `bun run generate-schemas` when docs directory is absent (runtime-only clone / public mirror behavior).\n    39→- Running runtime tests on Windows/macOS/Linux as CI does (matrix), focusing on any path separator assumptions (workspace paths can surface these).\n    40→\n    41→### A.6 Test patterns and conventions to follow\n    42→\n    43→- Keep runtime tests scoped to `source/hankweave/tests/**` (don’t intermix docs tests into the runtime suite).\n    44→- Prefer CI checks for docs build rather than introducing a new test runner/framework inside docs unless there’s already a pattern for it.\n    45→\n    46→### A.7 Conflicts or inconsistencies uncovered\n    47→\n    48→- The task instructions mentioned reviewing docs under `.../pages`, but the docs snapshot uses `nextra-site/content/` and `app/` (Nextra 4 + App Router). This primarily affects any “where to edit docs” guidance, not runtime tests.\n    49→\n    50→### A.8 New risks introduced\n    51→\n    52→- Lockfile convergence is the main operational risk:\n    53→  - CI will fail early if the consolidated `bun.lock` doesn’t match the workspace dependency graph.\n    54→  - Vercel install behavior may diverge from CI/local unless explicitly validated (see docs addendum).\n    55→\n    56→---\n    57→\n    58→## B) Concrete CI/CD update checklist (when implementing)\n    59→\n    60→1. Confirm CI still runs from monorepo root without changing working directories.\n    61→2. Add a docs build job (optional but recommended).\n    62→3. Consider `paths:` filtering to avoid running full runtime CI on docs-only changes (nice-to-have).\n    63→4. If public sync transforms `package.json`, verify the transformation removes any monorepo-only metadata (e.g., `\"workspaces\"`) so the public repo doesn’t accidentally become “workspace-shaped”.\n    64→\n    65→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"f87d5013-6289-4cdd-a4b1-2f5939cf5123","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/codex/addendum-1.md","content":"# Addendum 1: Tests + CI/CD Implications\n\nThis addendum is meant to be read alongside `plan/rough-workspace/codex/plan.md`.\n\n## A) Thinking (what changed after reading tests)\n\n### A.1 What supplementary materials did I review?\n\n- Runtime test suite under `source/hankweave/tests/` (unit/integration/e2e/cross-runtime scaffolding).\n- Runtime CI composite actions and workflows under `source/hankweave/.github/`.\n\n### A.2 What do these materials add that the main plan didn’t have?\n\n- CI runs tests from repo root via composite actions and assumes `bun install --frozen-lockfile` succeeds at the repo root. `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`.\n- CI’s test action runs explicit runtime-only directories (`tests/unit`, `tests/integration`) and cross-runtime tests via scripts, so docs co-location should not directly expand the test scope unless we choose to add a docs build step. `source/hankweave/.github/actions/run-tests/action.yml:9`.\n\n### A.3 What existing tests are affected by the planned changes?\n\n- Expected: **no test code changes**, if runtime remains at repo root and docs is simply added as a subdirectory workspace.\n- Indirectly affected: anything that depends on `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and the shape of `bun.lock` after workspace convergence.\n\n### A.4 What new tests (or CI checks) should be added?\n\nRecommended minimal additions:\n\n1. **Docs build check (CI job)**\n   - Add a `docs-build` job that runs `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun run build` to prevent monorepo PRs from breaking docs silently.\n\n2. **Schema generation regression check (optional)**\n   - A lightweight check that `bun run generate-schemas` succeeds in the monorepo and updates both:\n     - runtime `schemas/`\n     - docs `public/schemas/`\n   - If schemas are committed, follow with `git diff --exit-code` to ensure the repo stays in sync.\n\n### A.5 Edge cases to cover in validation\n\n- `bun install --frozen-lockfile` from the monorepo root after merging lockfiles (this is what CI runs).\n- Running `bun run generate-schemas` when docs directory is absent (runtime-only clone / public mirror behavior).\n- Running runtime tests on Windows/macOS/Linux as CI does (matrix), focusing on any path separator assumptions (workspace paths can surface these).\n\n### A.6 Test patterns and conventions to follow\n\n- Keep runtime tests scoped to `source/hankweave/tests/**` (don’t intermix docs tests into the runtime suite).\n- Prefer CI checks for docs build rather than introducing a new test runner/framework inside docs unless there’s already a pattern for it.\n\n### A.7 Conflicts or inconsistencies uncovered\n\n- The task instructions mentioned reviewing docs under `.../pages`, but the docs snapshot uses `nextra-site/content/` and `app/` (Nextra 4 + App Router). This primarily affects any “where to edit docs” guidance, not runtime tests.\n\n### A.8 New risks introduced\n\n- Lockfile convergence is the main operational risk:\n  - CI will fail early if the consolidated `bun.lock` doesn’t match the workspace dependency graph.\n  - Vercel install behavior may diverge from CI/local unless explicitly validated (see docs addendum).\n\n---\n\n## B) Concrete CI/CD update checklist (when implementing)\n\n1. Confirm CI still runs from monorepo root without changing working directories.\n2. Add a docs build job (optional but recommended).\n3. Consider `paths:` filtering to avoid running full runtime CI on docs-only changes (nice-to-have).\n4. If public sync transforms `package.json`, verify the transformation removes any monorepo-only metadata (e.g., `\"workspaces\"`) so the public repo doesn’t accidentally become “workspace-shaped”.\n\n","numLines":65,"startLine":1,"totalLines":65}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:57.888Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BxUpdE2N9V9ddnJzvcYyaS","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Hankweave Monorepo Migration Plan\n     2→\n     3→## 0) Thinking (inputs, constraints, and why this plan looks like this)\n     4→\n     5→### 0.1 What information am I making decisions with access to?\n     6→\n     7→**I can see:**\n     8→\n     9→- The task spec in `plan/task.md` and `read_only_data_source/task.md`.\n    10→- A full snapshot of the runtime codebase under `source/hankweave/`, including:\n    11→  - Runtime code (`source/hankweave/server/`), scripts (`source/hankweave/scripts/`), tests (`source/hankweave/tests/`), and CI workflows (`source/hankweave/.github/`).\n    12→- A full snapshot of the docs repo under `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` (Nextra/Next.js project root).\n    13→\n    14→**I cannot see:**\n    15→\n    16→- The actual GitHub repos (branch protections, secrets, default branches, Vercel project settings, or the real commit history), except what’s implied by files in `source/`.\n    17→- The public mirror repo (`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime`) contents beyond what is described in `plan/task.md`.\n    18→- Any external ticket/PR context (Linear, GitHub issues, etc.).\n    19→\n    20→### 0.2 What does this task imply in the broader code context?\n    21→\n    22→- The runtime already contains scripts and CI that implicitly assume “runtime repo root” is the working directory (e.g., build scripts use relative entrypoints and `import.meta.dir` paths). Example: `source/hankweave/scripts/build.ts:15` and `source/hankweave/scripts/build.ts:31`.\n    23→- The docs site is a full Next.js app with its own lockfile and dependencies today; merging it into the runtime repo will change dependency installation behavior unless we intentionally keep separate installs.\n    24→- There is a concrete existing cross-repo link: schema generation writes into docs `public/schemas/`, but the current path assumes sibling checkouts. `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts:24`–`32`.\n    25→\n    26→### 0.3 Conventions and patterns observed that matter here\n    27→\n    28→- Runtime is Bun + TypeScript ESM (`\"type\": \"module\"`) and uses explicit `.js` in local import specifiers. Example: `source/hankweave/server/index.ts:6`–`38`.\n    29→- Runtime utilities read version metadata by resolving `../package.json` relative to `server/utils.ts`, which assumes the runtime package’s internal layout stays stable. `source/hankweave/server/utils.ts:402`–`410`.\n    30→- CI uses composite actions and runs `bun install --frozen-lockfile` from repo root. `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:32`–`35`.\n    31→- Docs project root is `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` (not the outer `source/hankweave-docs/`), and docs content lives under `content/` (not `pages/`). Example: `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/content/reference/configuration.mdx:1`.\n    32→\n    33→### 0.4 Blast radius (most affected areas)\n    34→\n    35→High impact:\n    36→\n    37→- Repo root structure (where docs lands).\n    38→- Runtime `package.json` (add workspaces).\n    39→- Schema generation script path update (`scripts/generate-schemas.ts`).\n    40→- Public sync and packaging safety nets (`scripts/sync-public.ts`, plus potentially `sync-public.yml` assumptions).\n    41→- Dependency locking (converging `bun.lock` files).\n    42→\n    43→Medium impact:\n    44→\n    45→- CI time (installing docs dependencies too).\n    46→- Vercel configuration/root directory for docs deploy.\n    47→\n    48→Low impact (ideally unchanged if we choose the right structure):\n    49→\n    50→- Runtime server code (`source/hankweave/server/`).\n    51→- Runtime tests (`source/hankweave/tests/`).\n    52→- Runtime release workflow shape (publish, binaries) assuming runtime stays at root.\n    53→\n    54→### 0.5 Key judgement calls + recommendations\n    55→\n    56→1) **Monorepo structure**\n    57→\n    58→- **Recommendation:** keep the runtime at the repo root, and import docs as a subdirectory workspace (e.g. `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`), rather than moving the runtime into `packages/`.\n    59→- Why: the runtime build/release/CI scripts assume root-level paths; moving the runtime would require many coordinated updates across scripts and workflows (e.g., `source/hankweave/scripts/build.ts:31`, `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml:40`–`76`).\n    60→\n    61→2) **Bun workspaces vs additional tooling**\n    62→\n    63→- **Recommendation:** start with plain Bun workspaces only. Add Turborepo/Nx later if/when task orchestration/caching is actually painful.\n    64→\n    65→3) **Public mirror safety**\n    66→\n    67→- **Recommendation:** treat docs as “private-only content” for the public mirror. Update `scripts/sync-public.ts` to exclude the docs directory (and remove workspace metadata from public `package.json` if it becomes confusing).\n    68→\n    69→4) **Git history**\n    70→\n    71→- **Recommendation:** use `git subtree` (or `git filter-repo` + merge) to preserve docs history “where practical”, but keep the runtime repo as the destination to avoid redoing CI/secrets/branch rules.\n    72→\n    73→### 0.6 Where to draw the line (avoid over/under-engineering)\n    74→\n    75→- Do **not** reorganize the runtime into a conventional `packages/` layout; it’s high churn for little immediate benefit.\n    76→- Do **not** introduce a task runner (turbo/nx) until there’s a real need.\n    77→- Do **do** the minimum needed to:\n    78→  - co-locate code, docs, and schema generation reliably\n    79→  - keep CI/release working\n    80→  - keep the public mirror clean and safe\n    81→\n    82→---\n    83→\n    84→## 1) The Main Task\n    85→\n    86→Migrate Hankweave from its current multi-repo structure into a single monorepo that includes:\n    87→\n    88→- the runtime (`source/hankweave/` today)\n    89→- the docs site (`source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` today)\n    90→- any related tooling that is currently “assumed colocated” (schema generation, release/sync scripts, CI)\n    91→\n    92→…while preserving package boundaries (workspaces), keeping CI/release functioning, and keeping the public mirror behavior correct.\n    93→\n    94→---\n    95→\n    96→## 2) Information Available (planning agent vs now)\n    97→\n    98→The upstream task authoring in `plan/task.md` already identifies the cross-repo schema write and outlines open questions. This plan refines those questions based on what’s actually in the runtime/scripts/workflows/docs snapshot, and narrows the migration to the smallest set of changes that:\n    99→\n   100→- makes cross-cutting changes (runtime + docs) atomic\n   101→- avoids breaking release/sync flows\n   102→- avoids a large path-rewrite refactor\n   103→\n   104→---\n   105→\n   106→## 3) Sources and Related Things\n   107→\n   108→### Task sources\n   109→\n   110→- `plan/task.md`\n   111→- `read_only_data_source/task.md`\n   112→\n   113→### Key code references (for migration)\n   114→\n   115→- Schema generation’s docs path assumption: `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts:24`\n   116→- Runtime build assumes root paths: `source/hankweave/scripts/build.ts:31`\n   117→- Runtime metadata reads root `package.json`: `source/hankweave/server/utils.ts:404`\n   118→- CI installs via Bun from repo root: `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`\n   119→- Release workflow reads root `package.json` and runs `npm publish`: `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml:40`\n   120→- Public sync excludes internal dirs via `STRIP_DIRS`: `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts:28`\n   121→\n   122→---\n   123→\n   124→## 4) Conventions and Patterns to Follow\n   125→\n   126→- Keep runtime layout stable (minimize moving files under `source/hankweave/`).\n   127→- Keep `.github/` at the repo root so composite actions keep working (they’re referenced as `./.github/actions/...` in workflows).\n   128→- Keep docs as a workspace rooted where its `package.json` lives (currently `nextra-site/`).\n   129→- Prefer explicit, single-purpose changes (path fixes + workspace config + sync safety), rather than large reorganizations.\n   130→\n   131→---\n   132→\n   133→## 5) Decision Points and Trade-offs\n   134→\n   135→### 5.1 Directory layout (recommended)\n   136→\n   137→**Recommended target repo layout (conceptual):**\n   138→\n   139→```text\n   140→<monorepo-root>/                      # remains the runtime repo root\n   141→├── package.json                      # runtime package.json + workspaces config\n   142→├── bun.lock                          # single workspace lockfile\n   143→├── server/                           # runtime\n   144→├── scripts/                          # runtime scripts (release/sync/build/schema gen)\n   145→├── tests/                            # runtime tests\n   146→├── hankweave-docs/\n   147→│   └── nextra-site/                  # docs workspace (unchanged from current docs repo)\n   148→│       ├── package.json\n   149→│       ├── app/\n   150→│       ├── content/\n   151→│       └── public/\n   152→└── .github/\n   153→```\n   154→\n   155→**Why this layout:**\n   156→\n   157→- Minimizes runtime path changes (build scripts, workflows, and metadata resolution keep working).\n   158→- Minimizes docs changes (keep `nextra-site/` as the Next/Nextra project root).\n   159→- Makes schema generation path a simple “one-level up then into docs” update.\n   160→\n   161→Alternative layouts (not recommended initially):\n   162→\n   163→- Move runtime into `packages/runtime/` (large blast radius: scripts/workflows/paths).\n   164→- Flatten docs into `docs/` (more churn moving directories, though still viable later).\n   165→\n   166→### 5.2 Workspaces and public sync\n   167→\n   168→Adding `\"workspaces\"` to the runtime `package.json` is the cleanest Bun-native approach, but it has an interaction with the public mirror:\n   169→\n   170→- The public mirror snapshot will not include docs.\n   171→- The public `package.json` could still contain `\"workspaces\"` pointing to missing paths unless we strip it in `scripts/sync-public.ts`.\n   172→\n   173→**Recommendation:** remove `\"workspaces\"` from `package.json` during public snapshot transformation to reduce confusion and minimize “missing workspace path” edge cases.\n   174→\n   175→---\n   176→\n   177→## 6) Actual Implementation Plan (step-by-step)\n   178→\n   179→### Phase 0 — Confirm target repo + guardrails\n   180→\n   181→1. Decide the destination repo:\n   182→   - Prefer converting the private runtime repo into the monorepo (keeps existing CI, secrets, release tags).\n   183→2. Decide docs placement:\n   184→   - Recommended: `<root>/hankweave-docs/nextra-site` (matching current docs repo shape).\n   185→3. Decide what remains public:\n   186→   - Public mirror continues to contain only runtime (as today), not docs.\n   187→\n   188→### Phase 1 — Bring docs into the repo (preserving history where practical)\n   189→\n   190→1. Import docs repo as a subtree into `hankweave-docs/` (preserves history).\n   191→   - If history preservation is not practical, perform a clean copy and start fresh history for docs in the monorepo.\n   192→2. Ensure `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json` remains unchanged to avoid churn.\n   193→\n   194→### Phase 2 — Configure Bun workspaces + lockfile convergence\n   195→\n   196→1. Update the runtime root `package.json` to include a Bun-compatible workspaces list, e.g.:\n   197→   - `\"workspaces\": [\"hankweave-docs/nextra-site\"]`\n   198→2. Remove the docs-local `bun.lock` (or allow Bun to supersede it) and generate a single root `bun.lock` via `bun install` at the monorepo root.\n   199→3. Confirm CI’s `bun install --frozen-lockfile` still succeeds after lockfile convergence. `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`.\n   200→\n   201→### Phase 3 — Fix cross-package schema generation path (the original friction point)\n   202→\n   203→1. Update `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` to point inside the monorepo rather than a sibling checkout.\n   204→   - Current reference: `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts:24`–`32`.\n   205→   - Target should resolve to `<root>/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas`.\n   206→2. Keep the “docs optional” behavior (only write to docs if the directory exists) so runtime-only clones still work.\n   207→\n   208→### Phase 4 — Keep public mirror clean (sync script + exclusions)\n   209→\n   210→1. Update snapshot exclusions to omit docs from public sync:\n   211→   - `scripts/sync-public.ts` `STRIP_DIRS` should include `hankweave-docs` (or whatever directory you pick). `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts:28`.\n   212→2. Update `transformPackageJson()` for the public repo:\n   213→   - delete `workspaces` (and any future monorepo-only fields) from the public `package.json`.\n   214→3. Validate `sync-public.yml` still runs without changes (it executes `bun private/scripts/sync-public.ts ...` and relies on the script’s exclusion list). `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/sync-public.yml:84`.\n   215→\n   216→### Phase 5 — CI/CD adjustments (minimal first, optional enhancements second)\n   217→\n   218→1. Keep runtime CI jobs running from repo root (no path changes expected if runtime stays at root).\n   219→2. Optional: add a `docs-build` CI job that runs on PRs to ensure docs don’t silently break:\n   220→   - `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun run build`\n   221→3. Optional: use `paths:` filters so docs-only changes don’t run the full runtime matrix.\n   222→\n   223→### Phase 6 — Docs deployment (Vercel) migration\n   224→\n   225→1. Reconfigure the Vercel project “Root Directory” to `hankweave-docs/nextra-site`.\n   226→2. Confirm `vercel.json` still applies and that `bun install` uses the intended lockfile strategy.\n   227→3. If Vercel can’t reliably install workspaces from a subdirectory, switch to:\n   228→   - Vercel root = monorepo root\n   229→   - build command = `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun run build`\n   230→   - output = Next default (Vercel detects Next when run in the proper directory)\n   231→\n   232→### Phase 7 — Validation checklist (local + CI)\n   233→\n   234→From monorepo root:\n   235→\n   236→- `bun install`\n   237→- `bun run generate-schemas` (confirm it writes to runtime `schemas/` and docs `public/schemas/`)\n   238→- `bun run lint`\n   239→- `bun run tc`\n   240→- `bun test tests/unit` and `bun test tests/integration` (CI mirrors this) `source/hankweave/.github/actions/run-tests/action.yml:9`.\n   241→\n   242→From docs workspace:\n   243→\n   244→- `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun install` (confirm it behaves as intended under workspaces)\n   245→- `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun run build`\n   246→\n   247→---\n   248→\n   249→## 7) Testing Considerations (summary)\n   250→\n   251→- Runtime tests should remain unaffected if runtime stays at root.\n   252→- The biggest risk is “install + lockfile + workspace” behavior in CI and Vercel; treat that as the main validation focus.\n   253→- Consider adding a docs build step in CI as a regression guard.\n   254→\n   255→(See `plan/rough-workspace/codex/addendum-1.md` for the detailed test addendum.)\n   256→\n   257→---\n   258→\n   259→## 8) Documentation Considerations (summary)\n   260→\n   261→- Add a monorepo-focused contributor doc or README section covering:\n   262→  - how to run runtime vs docs\n   263→  - how schema generation updates docs schemas\n   264→  - release/public-sync implications (docs not mirrored publicly)\n   265→- Confirm doc content references `content/` (Nextra 4) rather than legacy `pages/`.\n   266→\n   267→(See `plan/rough-workspace/codex/addendum-2.md` for the docs addendum.)\n   268→\n   269→---\n   270→\n   271→## 9) Additional Resources\n   272→\n   273→- Bun workspaces: https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces\n   274→- Bun catalogs (optional): https://bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs\n   275→- Git subtree merges: https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges\n   276→\n   277→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"e18a0bf0-ac00-402d-9b51-d44ca56de91b","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/codex/plan.md","content":"# Hankweave Monorepo Migration Plan\n\n## 0) Thinking (inputs, constraints, and why this plan looks like this)\n\n### 0.1 What information am I making decisions with access to?\n\n**I can see:**\n\n- The task spec in `plan/task.md` and `read_only_data_source/task.md`.\n- A full snapshot of the runtime codebase under `source/hankweave/`, including:\n  - Runtime code (`source/hankweave/server/`), scripts (`source/hankweave/scripts/`), tests (`source/hankweave/tests/`), and CI workflows (`source/hankweave/.github/`).\n- A full snapshot of the docs repo under `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` (Nextra/Next.js project root).\n\n**I cannot see:**\n\n- The actual GitHub repos (branch protections, secrets, default branches, Vercel project settings, or the real commit history), except what’s implied by files in `source/`.\n- The public mirror repo (`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime`) contents beyond what is described in `plan/task.md`.\n- Any external ticket/PR context (Linear, GitHub issues, etc.).\n\n### 0.2 What does this task imply in the broader code context?\n\n- The runtime already contains scripts and CI that implicitly assume “runtime repo root” is the working directory (e.g., build scripts use relative entrypoints and `import.meta.dir` paths). Example: `source/hankweave/scripts/build.ts:15` and `source/hankweave/scripts/build.ts:31`.\n- The docs site is a full Next.js app with its own lockfile and dependencies today; merging it into the runtime repo will change dependency installation behavior unless we intentionally keep separate installs.\n- There is a concrete existing cross-repo link: schema generation writes into docs `public/schemas/`, but the current path assumes sibling checkouts. `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts:24`–`32`.\n\n### 0.3 Conventions and patterns observed that matter here\n\n- Runtime is Bun + TypeScript ESM (`\"type\": \"module\"`) and uses explicit `.js` in local import specifiers. Example: `source/hankweave/server/index.ts:6`–`38`.\n- Runtime utilities read version metadata by resolving `../package.json` relative to `server/utils.ts`, which assumes the runtime package’s internal layout stays stable. `source/hankweave/server/utils.ts:402`–`410`.\n- CI uses composite actions and runs `bun install --frozen-lockfile` from repo root. `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:32`–`35`.\n- Docs project root is `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` (not the outer `source/hankweave-docs/`), and docs content lives under `content/` (not `pages/`). Example: `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/content/reference/configuration.mdx:1`.\n\n### 0.4 Blast radius (most affected areas)\n\nHigh impact:\n\n- Repo root structure (where docs lands).\n- Runtime `package.json` (add workspaces).\n- Schema generation script path update (`scripts/generate-schemas.ts`).\n- Public sync and packaging safety nets (`scripts/sync-public.ts`, plus potentially `sync-public.yml` assumptions).\n- Dependency locking (converging `bun.lock` files).\n\nMedium impact:\n\n- CI time (installing docs dependencies too).\n- Vercel configuration/root directory for docs deploy.\n\nLow impact (ideally unchanged if we choose the right structure):\n\n- Runtime server code (`source/hankweave/server/`).\n- Runtime tests (`source/hankweave/tests/`).\n- Runtime release workflow shape (publish, binaries) assuming runtime stays at root.\n\n### 0.5 Key judgement calls + recommendations\n\n1) **Monorepo structure**\n\n- **Recommendation:** keep the runtime at the repo root, and import docs as a subdirectory workspace (e.g. `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`), rather than moving the runtime into `packages/`.\n- Why: the runtime build/release/CI scripts assume root-level paths; moving the runtime would require many coordinated updates across scripts and workflows (e.g., `source/hankweave/scripts/build.ts:31`, `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml:40`–`76`).\n\n2) **Bun workspaces vs additional tooling**\n\n- **Recommendation:** start with plain Bun workspaces only. Add Turborepo/Nx later if/when task orchestration/caching is actually painful.\n\n3) **Public mirror safety**\n\n- **Recommendation:** treat docs as “private-only content” for the public mirror. Update `scripts/sync-public.ts` to exclude the docs directory (and remove workspace metadata from public `package.json` if it becomes confusing).\n\n4) **Git history**\n\n- **Recommendation:** use `git subtree` (or `git filter-repo` + merge) to preserve docs history “where practical”, but keep the runtime repo as the destination to avoid redoing CI/secrets/branch rules.\n\n### 0.6 Where to draw the line (avoid over/under-engineering)\n\n- Do **not** reorganize the runtime into a conventional `packages/` layout; it’s high churn for little immediate benefit.\n- Do **not** introduce a task runner (turbo/nx) until there’s a real need.\n- Do **do** the minimum needed to:\n  - co-locate code, docs, and schema generation reliably\n  - keep CI/release working\n  - keep the public mirror clean and safe\n\n---\n\n## 1) The Main Task\n\nMigrate Hankweave from its current multi-repo structure into a single monorepo that includes:\n\n- the runtime (`source/hankweave/` today)\n- the docs site (`source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` today)\n- any related tooling that is currently “assumed colocated” (schema generation, release/sync scripts, CI)\n\n…while preserving package boundaries (workspaces), keeping CI/release functioning, and keeping the public mirror behavior correct.\n\n---\n\n## 2) Information Available (planning agent vs now)\n\nThe upstream task authoring in `plan/task.md` already identifies the cross-repo schema write and outlines open questions. This plan refines those questions based on what’s actually in the runtime/scripts/workflows/docs snapshot, and narrows the migration to the smallest set of changes that:\n\n- makes cross-cutting changes (runtime + docs) atomic\n- avoids breaking release/sync flows\n- avoids a large path-rewrite refactor\n\n---\n\n## 3) Sources and Related Things\n\n### Task sources\n\n- `plan/task.md`\n- `read_only_data_source/task.md`\n\n### Key code references (for migration)\n\n- Schema generation’s docs path assumption: `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts:24`\n- Runtime build assumes root paths: `source/hankweave/scripts/build.ts:31`\n- Runtime metadata reads root `package.json`: `source/hankweave/server/utils.ts:404`\n- CI installs via Bun from repo root: `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`\n- Release workflow reads root `package.json` and runs `npm publish`: `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml:40`\n- Public sync excludes internal dirs via `STRIP_DIRS`: `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts:28`\n\n---\n\n## 4) Conventions and Patterns to Follow\n\n- Keep runtime layout stable (minimize moving files under `source/hankweave/`).\n- Keep `.github/` at the repo root so composite actions keep working (they’re referenced as `./.github/actions/...` in workflows).\n- Keep docs as a workspace rooted where its `package.json` lives (currently `nextra-site/`).\n- Prefer explicit, single-purpose changes (path fixes + workspace config + sync safety), rather than large reorganizations.\n\n---\n\n## 5) Decision Points and Trade-offs\n\n### 5.1 Directory layout (recommended)\n\n**Recommended target repo layout (conceptual):**\n\n```text\n<monorepo-root>/                      # remains the runtime repo root\n├── package.json                      # runtime package.json + workspaces config\n├── bun.lock                          # single workspace lockfile\n├── server/                           # runtime\n├── scripts/                          # runtime scripts (release/sync/build/schema gen)\n├── tests/                            # runtime tests\n├── hankweave-docs/\n│   └── nextra-site/                  # docs workspace (unchanged from current docs repo)\n│       ├── package.json\n│       ├── app/\n│       ├── content/\n│       └── public/\n└── .github/\n```\n\n**Why this layout:**\n\n- Minimizes runtime path changes (build scripts, workflows, and metadata resolution keep working).\n- Minimizes docs changes (keep `nextra-site/` as the Next/Nextra project root).\n- Makes schema generation path a simple “one-level up then into docs” update.\n\nAlternative layouts (not recommended initially):\n\n- Move runtime into `packages/runtime/` (large blast radius: scripts/workflows/paths).\n- Flatten docs into `docs/` (more churn moving directories, though still viable later).\n\n### 5.2 Workspaces and public sync\n\nAdding `\"workspaces\"` to the runtime `package.json` is the cleanest Bun-native approach, but it has an interaction with the public mirror:\n\n- The public mirror snapshot will not include docs.\n- The public `package.json` could still contain `\"workspaces\"` pointing to missing paths unless we strip it in `scripts/sync-public.ts`.\n\n**Recommendation:** remove `\"workspaces\"` from `package.json` during public snapshot transformation to reduce confusion and minimize “missing workspace path” edge cases.\n\n---\n\n## 6) Actual Implementation Plan (step-by-step)\n\n### Phase 0 — Confirm target repo + guardrails\n\n1. Decide the destination repo:\n   - Prefer converting the private runtime repo into the monorepo (keeps existing CI, secrets, release tags).\n2. Decide docs placement:\n   - Recommended: `<root>/hankweave-docs/nextra-site` (matching current docs repo shape).\n3. Decide what remains public:\n   - Public mirror continues to contain only runtime (as today), not docs.\n\n### Phase 1 — Bring docs into the repo (preserving history where practical)\n\n1. Import docs repo as a subtree into `hankweave-docs/` (preserves history).\n   - If history preservation is not practical, perform a clean copy and start fresh history for docs in the monorepo.\n2. Ensure `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json` remains unchanged to avoid churn.\n\n### Phase 2 — Configure Bun workspaces + lockfile convergence\n\n1. Update the runtime root `package.json` to include a Bun-compatible workspaces list, e.g.:\n   - `\"workspaces\": [\"hankweave-docs/nextra-site\"]`\n2. Remove the docs-local `bun.lock` (or allow Bun to supersede it) and generate a single root `bun.lock` via `bun install` at the monorepo root.\n3. Confirm CI’s `bun install --frozen-lockfile` still succeeds after lockfile convergence. `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`.\n\n### Phase 3 — Fix cross-package schema generation path (the original friction point)\n\n1. Update `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` to point inside the monorepo rather than a sibling checkout.\n   - Current reference: `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts:24`–`32`.\n   - Target should resolve to `<root>/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas`.\n2. Keep the “docs optional” behavior (only write to docs if the directory exists) so runtime-only clones still work.\n\n### Phase 4 — Keep public mirror clean (sync script + exclusions)\n\n1. Update snapshot exclusions to omit docs from public sync:\n   - `scripts/sync-public.ts` `STRIP_DIRS` should include `hankweave-docs` (or whatever directory you pick). `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts:28`.\n2. Update `transformPackageJson()` for the public repo:\n   - delete `workspaces` (and any future monorepo-only fields) from the public `package.json`.\n3. Validate `sync-public.yml` still runs without changes (it executes `bun private/scripts/sync-public.ts ...` and relies on the script’s exclusion list). `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/sync-public.yml:84`.\n\n### Phase 5 — CI/CD adjustments (minimal first, optional enhancements second)\n\n1. Keep runtime CI jobs running from repo root (no path changes expected if runtime stays at root).\n2. Optional: add a `docs-build` CI job that runs on PRs to ensure docs don’t silently break:\n   - `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun run build`\n3. Optional: use `paths:` filters so docs-only changes don’t run the full runtime matrix.\n\n### Phase 6 — Docs deployment (Vercel) migration\n\n1. Reconfigure the Vercel project “Root Directory” to `hankweave-docs/nextra-site`.\n2. Confirm `vercel.json` still applies and that `bun install` uses the intended lockfile strategy.\n3. If Vercel can’t reliably install workspaces from a subdirectory, switch to:\n   - Vercel root = monorepo root\n   - build command = `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun run build`\n   - output = Next default (Vercel detects Next when run in the proper directory)\n\n### Phase 7 — Validation checklist (local + CI)\n\nFrom monorepo root:\n\n- `bun install`\n- `bun run generate-schemas` (confirm it writes to runtime `schemas/` and docs `public/schemas/`)\n- `bun run lint`\n- `bun run tc`\n- `bun test tests/unit` and `bun test tests/integration` (CI mirrors this) `source/hankweave/.github/actions/run-tests/action.yml:9`.\n\nFrom docs workspace:\n\n- `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun install` (confirm it behaves as intended under workspaces)\n- `cd hankweave-docs/nextra-site && bun run build`\n\n---\n\n## 7) Testing Considerations (summary)\n\n- Runtime tests should remain unaffected if runtime stays at root.\n- The biggest risk is “install + lockfile + workspace” behavior in CI and Vercel; treat that as the main validation focus.\n- Consider adding a docs build step in CI as a regression guard.\n\n(See `plan/rough-workspace/codex/addendum-1.md` for the detailed test addendum.)\n\n---\n\n## 8) Documentation Considerations (summary)\n\n- Add a monorepo-focused contributor doc or README section covering:\n  - how to run runtime vs docs\n  - how schema generation updates docs schemas\n  - release/public-sync implications (docs not mirrored publicly)\n- Confirm doc content references `content/` (Nextra 4) rather than legacy `pages/`.\n\n(See `plan/rough-workspace/codex/addendum-2.md` for the docs addendum.)\n\n---\n\n## 9) Additional Resources\n\n- Bun workspaces: https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces\n- Bun catalogs (optional): https://bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs\n- Git subtree merges: https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges\n\n","numLines":277,"startLine":1,"totalLines":277}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:57.905Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0183tZeptapYLoQHxwjeNbNG","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Addendum 2: Documentation + Deployment Implications\n     2→\n     3→This addendum is meant to be read alongside `plan/rough-workspace/codex/plan.md`.\n     4→\n     5→## A) Thinking (what changed after reading docs)\n     6→\n     7→### A.1 What supplementary materials did I review?\n     8→\n     9→- Docs repository snapshot under `source/hankweave-docs/`, focusing on:\n    10→  - Next/Nextra project root: `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`\n    11→  - Doc content: `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/content/`\n    12→  - Deployment config: `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json:2`\n    13→\n    14→### A.2 What do these materials add that the main plan didn’t have?\n    15→\n    16→- The docs site is **not** rooted at `hankweave-docs/`; it’s rooted at `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` (that’s where `package.json` lives).\n    17→- The docs use Nextra 4 + Next.js App Router conventions (`app/` + `content/`), so any migration notes must reference `content/` rather than a legacy `pages/` directory.\n    18→- The docs build includes a `postbuild` Pagefind step (`pagefind --site .next/server/app ...`) which assumes an App Router build output. `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json:8`.\n    19→\n    20→### A.3 What existing docs are affected by the monorepo change?\n    21→\n    22→- User-facing docs content under `content/` is mostly runtime-usage focused and should not need changes purely because the repos are colocated.\n    23→- Contributor-facing docs (README and any internal guidance) likely need updates to reflect:\n    24→  - new directory paths (docs live in-repo)\n    25→  - workspace install commands\n    26→  - where schemas come from and how they’re updated\n    27→\n    28→### A.4 What new docs should be written or updated?\n    29→\n    30→Recommended minimal doc updates:\n    31→\n    32→1. Update `source/hankweave-docs/README.md` to add “Local development in the monorepo”:\n    33→   - where the docs project lives (monorepo path)\n    34→   - how to install deps (root install vs docs-only install)\n    35→   - how to run `dev` and `build`\n    36→\n    37→2. Add/extend a monorepo root README section (runtime repo README or a new short root section) covering:\n    38→   - `bun install` from root (workspaces)\n    39→   - running runtime vs docs\n    40→   - schema generation writing into docs `public/schemas/`\n    41→\n    42→3. Optional: Add a short “Contributing” page in the docs site itself (under `content/`) if contributors use the docs site as their entry point.\n    43→\n    44→### A.5 Optional flourishes that improve UX/maintainability\n    45→\n    46→- Add a dedicated “Monorepo” section to internal SOPs (if contributors use `standard-operating-procedures.md`) describing:\n    47→  - workspace structure\n    48→  - public sync rules (“docs not mirrored publicly”)\n    49→  - Vercel configuration expectations\n    50→\n    51→### A.6 Conflicts or inconsistencies revealed by docs\n    52→\n    53→- The task process instructions referenced `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/pages`, but the repo uses `content/` and `app/`. Any planning/checklists should treat `content/` as the authoritative doc source.\n    54→\n    55→### A.7 New implications/risks\n    56→\n    57→- Vercel configuration needs deliberate validation in a monorepo:\n    58→  - If Vercel runs `bun install` from a subdirectory root (`hankweave-docs/nextra-site`), confirm Bun workspaces behave as intended (lockfile location, workspace resolution).\n    59→  - If not reliable, adjust Vercel to install from monorepo root and build from the docs directory.\n    60→\n    61→### A.8 Plan assumptions to revisit\n    62→\n    63→- If Vercel cannot work cleanly with Bun workspaces in the chosen layout, we may need to:\n    64→  - adjust `vercel.json` install/build commands, or\n    65→  - run Vercel from monorepo root and explicitly `cd` for build.\n    66→\n    67→---\n    68→\n    69→## B) Concrete docs/deploy update checklist (when implementing)\n    70→\n    71→1. Decide docs location in the monorepo (`hankweave-docs/nextra-site` recommended).\n    72→2. Confirm schema hosting path stays `public/schemas/` and update the generator script accordingly (monorepo-relative path).\n    73→3. Reconfigure Vercel “Root Directory” to the new in-repo docs path.\n    74→4. Validate docs build:\n    75→   - `bun run build`\n    76→   - `postbuild` Pagefind output exists in `public/_pagefind`\n    77→5. Update contributor docs (README or internal docs) with monorepo commands and paths.\n    78→\n    79→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"4763b53c-0078-48e8-8904-7aa589096e40","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/codex/addendum-2.md","content":"# Addendum 2: Documentation + Deployment Implications\n\nThis addendum is meant to be read alongside `plan/rough-workspace/codex/plan.md`.\n\n## A) Thinking (what changed after reading docs)\n\n### A.1 What supplementary materials did I review?\n\n- Docs repository snapshot under `source/hankweave-docs/`, focusing on:\n  - Next/Nextra project root: `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`\n  - Doc content: `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/content/`\n  - Deployment config: `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json:2`\n\n### A.2 What do these materials add that the main plan didn’t have?\n\n- The docs site is **not** rooted at `hankweave-docs/`; it’s rooted at `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` (that’s where `package.json` lives).\n- The docs use Nextra 4 + Next.js App Router conventions (`app/` + `content/`), so any migration notes must reference `content/` rather than a legacy `pages/` directory.\n- The docs build includes a `postbuild` Pagefind step (`pagefind --site .next/server/app ...`) which assumes an App Router build output. `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json:8`.\n\n### A.3 What existing docs are affected by the monorepo change?\n\n- User-facing docs content under `content/` is mostly runtime-usage focused and should not need changes purely because the repos are colocated.\n- Contributor-facing docs (README and any internal guidance) likely need updates to reflect:\n  - new directory paths (docs live in-repo)\n  - workspace install commands\n  - where schemas come from and how they’re updated\n\n### A.4 What new docs should be written or updated?\n\nRecommended minimal doc updates:\n\n1. Update `source/hankweave-docs/README.md` to add “Local development in the monorepo”:\n   - where the docs project lives (monorepo path)\n   - how to install deps (root install vs docs-only install)\n   - how to run `dev` and `build`\n\n2. Add/extend a monorepo root README section (runtime repo README or a new short root section) covering:\n   - `bun install` from root (workspaces)\n   - running runtime vs docs\n   - schema generation writing into docs `public/schemas/`\n\n3. Optional: Add a short “Contributing” page in the docs site itself (under `content/`) if contributors use the docs site as their entry point.\n\n### A.5 Optional flourishes that improve UX/maintainability\n\n- Add a dedicated “Monorepo” section to internal SOPs (if contributors use `standard-operating-procedures.md`) describing:\n  - workspace structure\n  - public sync rules (“docs not mirrored publicly”)\n  - Vercel configuration expectations\n\n### A.6 Conflicts or inconsistencies revealed by docs\n\n- The task process instructions referenced `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/pages`, but the repo uses `content/` and `app/`. Any planning/checklists should treat `content/` as the authoritative doc source.\n\n### A.7 New implications/risks\n\n- Vercel configuration needs deliberate validation in a monorepo:\n  - If Vercel runs `bun install` from a subdirectory root (`hankweave-docs/nextra-site`), confirm Bun workspaces behave as intended (lockfile location, workspace resolution).\n  - If not reliable, adjust Vercel to install from monorepo root and build from the docs directory.\n\n### A.8 Plan assumptions to revisit\n\n- If Vercel cannot work cleanly with Bun workspaces in the chosen layout, we may need to:\n  - adjust `vercel.json` install/build commands, or\n  - run Vercel from monorepo root and explicitly `cd` for build.\n\n---\n\n## B) Concrete docs/deploy update checklist (when implementing)\n\n1. Decide docs location in the monorepo (`hankweave-docs/nextra-site` recommended).\n2. Confirm schema hosting path stays `public/schemas/` and update the generator script accordingly (monorepo-relative path).\n3. Reconfigure Vercel “Root Directory” to the new in-repo docs path.\n4. Validate docs build:\n   - `bun run build`\n   - `postbuild` Pagefind output exists in `public/_pagefind`\n5. Update contributor docs (README or internal docs) with monorepo commands and paths.\n\n","numLines":79,"startLine":1,"totalLines":79}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:28:57.917Z"}
