{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UqxUhbpzfCRCCt2SQVGSWn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019nVFHHHN22YRa8AWuWxk31","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/log.md","offset":130},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5010,"cache_read_input_tokens":127425,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5010},"output_tokens":221,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"dcc33083-6beb-4a6f-8f78-9909f869136f","timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:38:21.995Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019nVFHHHN22YRa8AWuWxk31","type":"tool_result","content":"   130→The trade-off: the root `package.json` serves double duty. But Bun workspaces handles this fine — you can have `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` in the root and the root itself is also a package.\n   131→\n   132→### Sync-public impact\n   133→\n   134→With Option A (runtime at root), the sync-public.ts barely changes. The only new item is that the `docs/` directory needs to be added to the `STRIP_DIRS` list so it doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot. Everything else stays the same.\n   135→\n   136→### CI impact\n   137→\n   138→With Option A:\n   139→- `ci.yml` barely changes — the runtime is still at root, `bun install` installs all workspaces, tests run from root\n   140→- `release.yml` same thing\n   141→- Optional: Add path filtering so docs-only changes skip runtime tests (nice to have, not required initially)\n   142→\n   143→### generate-schemas.ts impact\n   144→\n   145→The `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path changes from `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas` to `../docs/public/schemas` (one level up from `scripts/`, then into `docs/`). This is actually simpler.\n   146→\n   147→## Questions Resolved\n   148→\n   149→1. **Full directory structure** — mapped exhaustively\n   150→2. **Script invocation** — all via `bun run <script>` or `bun scripts/<file>.ts`\n   151→3. **Sync-public strips** — STRIP_DIRS + STRIP_FILES documented, understood\n   152→4. **Test infrastructure** — bun:test, 65+ unit, 10+ integration, e2e with mock LLMs\n   153→5. **server/ depth** — 3-4 levels (server/sentinels/, server/llm/, server/types/, etc.)\n   154→6. **Docs structure** — Nextra 4 + Next.js 15, content/ for MDX, app/ for routes, components/ for custom\n   155→\n   156→## Questions Remaining\n   157→\n   158→1. **Should the outer hankweave-docs/ wrapper be kept?** — The docs repo has a root-level README, notes/, plan/ directory outside nextra-site/. These are dev-only files. Probably flatten: just bring nextra-site/ contents into docs/.\n   159→2. **Shared dev dependencies?** — Both projects use TypeScript. The docs use `@types/node` and `@types/react`. The runtime uses `@types/bun`. Bun catalogs could share TypeScript version, but it's not critical.\n   160→3. **Does the monorepo need its own README at root?** — The current runtime README is internal. The public one is in `public-release-files/`. Adding a monorepo root README could be helpful but isn't strictly necessary for the migration.\n   161→\n   162→---\n   163→\n   164→## Codex Pass Notes (2026-03-05)\n   165→\n   166→### Immediate Questions / Unknowns\n   167→\n   168→- Vercel + Bun workspaces: confirm whether Vercel running `bun install` inside the docs root will correctly detect the workspace root and use the monorepo `bun.lock`, or whether we need explicit `installCommand`/`buildCommand` that `cd`s to the monorepo root first.\n   169→- Public sync transform: if the monorepo adds `\"workspaces\"` to the runtime `package.json`, decide whether `scripts/sync-public.ts` should delete `\"workspaces\"` when transforming `package.json` for the public snapshot.\n   170→- Docs content location mismatch: task spec references `nextra-site/pages/`, but this snapshot uses `nextra-site/content/` + `app/` (Nextra 4 + App Router). Any migration doc updates should reference `content/`, not `pages/`.\n   171→\n   172→### Codebase Conventions Noted (relevant to migration choices)\n   173→\n   174→- Runtime code is strict ESM TypeScript with explicit `.js` import specifiers (e.g., `server/index.ts` imports `./utils.js`).\n   175→- Runtime reads `package.json` via a path relative to `server/utils.ts` (`../package.json`), which implicitly assumes the runtime package layout stays stable.\n   176→- Build scripts (`scripts/build.ts`, `scripts/build-executable.ts`) assume they run with CWD = runtime root (`entrypoints: [\"./server/index.ts\"]` and lots of `import.meta.dir`-relative paths).\n   177→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"b8503931-1180-477c-8a33-af323be0241a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/log.md","content":"The trade-off: the root `package.json` serves double duty. But Bun workspaces handles this fine — you can have `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` in the root and the root itself is also a package.\n\n### Sync-public impact\n\nWith Option A (runtime at root), the sync-public.ts barely changes. The only new item is that the `docs/` directory needs to be added to the `STRIP_DIRS` list so it doesn't end up in the public runtime snapshot. Everything else stays the same.\n\n### CI impact\n\nWith Option A:\n- `ci.yml` barely changes — the runtime is still at root, `bun install` installs all workspaces, tests run from root\n- `release.yml` same thing\n- Optional: Add path filtering so docs-only changes skip runtime tests (nice to have, not required initially)\n\n### generate-schemas.ts impact\n\nThe `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path changes from `../../hankweave-docs/nextra-site/public/schemas` to `../docs/public/schemas` (one level up from `scripts/`, then into `docs/`). This is actually simpler.\n\n## Questions Resolved\n\n1. **Full directory structure** — mapped exhaustively\n2. **Script invocation** — all via `bun run <script>` or `bun scripts/<file>.ts`\n3. **Sync-public strips** — STRIP_DIRS + STRIP_FILES documented, understood\n4. **Test infrastructure** — bun:test, 65+ unit, 10+ integration, e2e with mock LLMs\n5. **server/ depth** — 3-4 levels (server/sentinels/, server/llm/, server/types/, etc.)\n6. **Docs structure** — Nextra 4 + Next.js 15, content/ for MDX, app/ for routes, components/ for custom\n\n## Questions Remaining\n\n1. **Should the outer hankweave-docs/ wrapper be kept?** — The docs repo has a root-level README, notes/, plan/ directory outside nextra-site/. These are dev-only files. Probably flatten: just bring nextra-site/ contents into docs/.\n2. **Shared dev dependencies?** — Both projects use TypeScript. The docs use `@types/node` and `@types/react`. The runtime uses `@types/bun`. Bun catalogs could share TypeScript version, but it's not critical.\n3. **Does the monorepo need its own README at root?** — The current runtime README is internal. The public one is in `public-release-files/`. Adding a monorepo root README could be helpful but isn't strictly necessary for the migration.\n\n---\n\n## Codex Pass Notes (2026-03-05)\n\n### Immediate Questions / Unknowns\n\n- Vercel + Bun workspaces: confirm whether Vercel running `bun install` inside the docs root will correctly detect the workspace root and use the monorepo `bun.lock`, or whether we need explicit `installCommand`/`buildCommand` that `cd`s to the monorepo root first.\n- Public sync transform: if the monorepo adds `\"workspaces\"` to the runtime `package.json`, decide whether `scripts/sync-public.ts` should delete `\"workspaces\"` when transforming `package.json` for the public snapshot.\n- Docs content location mismatch: task spec references `nextra-site/pages/`, but this snapshot uses `nextra-site/content/` + `app/` (Nextra 4 + App Router). Any migration doc updates should reference `content/`, not `pages/`.\n\n### Codebase Conventions Noted (relevant to migration choices)\n\n- Runtime code is strict ESM TypeScript with explicit `.js` import specifiers (e.g., `server/index.ts` imports `./utils.js`).\n- Runtime reads `package.json` via a path relative to `server/utils.ts` (`../package.json`), which implicitly assumes the runtime package layout stays stable.\n- Build scripts (`scripts/build.ts`, `scripts/build-executable.ts`) assume they run with CWD = runtime root (`entrypoints: [\"./server/index.ts\"]` and lots of `import.meta.dir`-relative paths).\n","numLines":48,"startLine":130,"totalLines":177}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:38:22.409Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018Zz1Y1XgNfsP3creTXkoUt","type":"tool_result","content":"   250→```\n   251→\n   252→The `docsAvailable` check in `generate-schemas.ts` becomes permanently true (the `docs/` directory is always present in the monorepo). The check should be kept for safety but will never evaluate to false in normal operation.\n   253→\n   254→### Release flow for schemas\n   255→\n   256→1. Developer runs `bun run generate-schemas` (or it runs automatically during release)\n   257→2. Schemas are written to both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`\n   258→3. Both locations are committed together\n   259→4. When the runtime is synced to public, `schemas/` goes with it\n   260→5. When docs deploy to Vercel, `docs/public/schemas/` goes with it\n   261→6. Both consumers always have the latest schemas — no coordination needed\n   262→\n   263→This is the primary benefit of the monorepo: what was previously a fragile cross-repo file write is now a simple intra-repo operation that's always consistent.\n   264→\n   265→---\n   266→\n   267→## 6. Implications and risks from docs review\n   268→\n   269→### 6.1: `hankweave-docs` repo archival\n   270→\n   271→After the monorepo migration, the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` repo becomes orphaned. It should be:\n   272→1. Archived (read-only) on GitHub\n   273→2. README updated to say \"This repo has been merged into SouthBridgeAI/hankweave\"\n   274→3. Vercel disconnected (after the new Vercel connection is verified)\n   275→\n   276→### 6.2: The `notes/` and `plan/` directories\n   277→\n   278→The docs repo has `hankweave-docs/notes/` and `hankweave-docs/plan/` directories outside `nextra-site/`. These are internal planning documents. Options:\n   279→- **Discard**: They're historical and won't be needed going forward\n   280→- **Archive**: Copy them into `intermediates/` as a numbered entry (e.g., `intermediates/docs-migration/legacy-notes/`)\n   281→- **Include in docs/**: Keep them at `docs/notes/` and `docs/plan/` (they won't be built by Next.js since they're not in `content/` or `app/`)\n   282→\n   283→Recommendation: Discard. If the team wants them preserved, archive in `intermediates/`.\n   284→\n   285→### 6.3: Build environment variable\n   286→\n   287→The `vercel.json` contains:\n   288→```json\n   289→{\n   290→  \"build\": {\n   291→    \"env\": {\n   292→      \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\"\n   293→    }\n   294→  }\n   295→}\n   296→```\n   297→\n   298→This environment variable is specific to the docs Vercel build and will continue to work in the same `vercel.json` at `docs/vercel.json`. No change needed, but worth noting for awareness.\n   299→\n   300→### 6.4: No new docs pages needed\n   301→\n   302→The monorepo migration is purely internal infrastructure. No new user-facing documentation pages are needed. The guides, concepts, and reference pages all document Hankweave's behavior and interface, not its repository layout.\n   303→\n   304→---\n   305→\n   306→## 7. Assumptions revisited after docs review\n   307→\n   308→### Assumption: \"The docs have no dependency on the runtime package\"\n   309→\n   310→**Confirmed.** After reviewing the full docs source (layout.tsx, page components, MDX content), there are zero imports from `@southbridgeai/hankweave` or any runtime module. The only data flow is the schema JSON files generated by the runtime's script.\n   311→\n   312→### Assumption: \"Vercel can build the docs from a monorepo subdirectory\"\n   313→\n   314→**Mostly confirmed, with a caveat.** Vercel supports monorepos with root directory configuration. However, Bun workspaces require the root `package.json` to be accessible for workspace resolution. If Vercel only mounts the subdirectory, `bun install` in `docs/` might not find the root `package.json` and might treat `docs/` as a standalone project (which would still work — just without workspace hoisting).\n   315→\n   316→**Mitigation**: If this is an issue, set Vercel root directory to `/` and update the build command to `cd docs && bun run build`.\n   317→\n   318→### Assumption: \"No shared development dependencies worth consolidating\"\n   319→\n   320→**Confirmed.** The only shared dependency is `typescript`. The runtime uses Bun-specific APIs and `bun-types`, while the docs use React, Next.js, and Nextra. There's no benefit to Bun catalogs or shared configs at this point.\n   321→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"832a7962-4131-49ac-95a0-3338d1a5f92c","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/rough-workspace/opus/addendum-docs.md","content":"```\n\nThe `docsAvailable` check in `generate-schemas.ts` becomes permanently true (the `docs/` directory is always present in the monorepo). The check should be kept for safety but will never evaluate to false in normal operation.\n\n### Release flow for schemas\n\n1. Developer runs `bun run generate-schemas` (or it runs automatically during release)\n2. Schemas are written to both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`\n3. Both locations are committed together\n4. When the runtime is synced to public, `schemas/` goes with it\n5. When docs deploy to Vercel, `docs/public/schemas/` goes with it\n6. Both consumers always have the latest schemas — no coordination needed\n\nThis is the primary benefit of the monorepo: what was previously a fragile cross-repo file write is now a simple intra-repo operation that's always consistent.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Implications and risks from docs review\n\n### 6.1: `hankweave-docs` repo archival\n\nAfter the monorepo migration, the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` repo becomes orphaned. It should be:\n1. Archived (read-only) on GitHub\n2. README updated to say \"This repo has been merged into SouthBridgeAI/hankweave\"\n3. Vercel disconnected (after the new Vercel connection is verified)\n\n### 6.2: The `notes/` and `plan/` directories\n\nThe docs repo has `hankweave-docs/notes/` and `hankweave-docs/plan/` directories outside `nextra-site/`. These are internal planning documents. Options:\n- **Discard**: They're historical and won't be needed going forward\n- **Archive**: Copy them into `intermediates/` as a numbered entry (e.g., `intermediates/docs-migration/legacy-notes/`)\n- **Include in docs/**: Keep them at `docs/notes/` and `docs/plan/` (they won't be built by Next.js since they're not in `content/` or `app/`)\n\nRecommendation: Discard. If the team wants them preserved, archive in `intermediates/`.\n\n### 6.3: Build environment variable\n\nThe `vercel.json` contains:\n```json\n{\n  \"build\": {\n    \"env\": {\n      \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis environment variable is specific to the docs Vercel build and will continue to work in the same `vercel.json` at `docs/vercel.json`. No change needed, but worth noting for awareness.\n\n### 6.4: No new docs pages needed\n\nThe monorepo migration is purely internal infrastructure. No new user-facing documentation pages are needed. The guides, concepts, and reference pages all document Hankweave's behavior and interface, not its repository layout.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Assumptions revisited after docs review\n\n### Assumption: \"The docs have no dependency on the runtime package\"\n\n**Confirmed.** After reviewing the full docs source (layout.tsx, page components, MDX content), there are zero imports from `@southbridgeai/hankweave` or any runtime module. The only data flow is the schema JSON files generated by the runtime's script.\n\n### Assumption: \"Vercel can build the docs from a monorepo subdirectory\"\n\n**Mostly confirmed, with a caveat.** Vercel supports monorepos with root directory configuration. However, Bun workspaces require the root `package.json` to be accessible for workspace resolution. If Vercel only mounts the subdirectory, `bun install` in `docs/` might not find the root `package.json` and might treat `docs/` as a standalone project (which would still work — just without workspace hoisting).\n\n**Mitigation**: If this is an issue, set Vercel root directory to `/` and update the build command to `cd docs && bun run build`.\n\n### Assumption: \"No shared development dependencies worth consolidating\"\n\n**Confirmed.** The only shared dependency is `typescript`. The runtime uses Bun-specific APIs and `bun-types`, while the docs use React, Next.js, and Nextra. There's no benefit to Bun catalogs or shared configs at this point.\n","numLines":72,"startLine":250,"totalLines":321}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:38:22.435Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019s5x71GkkrJyvrJdfydNdC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good. Let me now check my merged plan against all the items I've noted. Let me scan through for anything missing.\n\nAfter re-reading all original plans, here are things I want to verify are in my merged plan:\n\n1. `transformPackageJson()` stripping `workspaces` - Yes, in Phase 3, Step 3.2\n2. Docs flattening decision - Yes, Decision 2\n3. `docsAvailable` permanence - Yes, in Phase 2, Step 2.1\n4. Vercel ignored build step - Yes, Phase 7, Step 7.3\n5. Old repo archival - Yes, Phase 8, Step 8.1\n6. CHANGELOG entry - Yes, Phase 8, Step 8.4\n7. Optional smoke test - Yes, in Testing section\n8. CI docs build job - Yes, in Testing section\n9. npm pack verification - Yes, Phase 6, Step 6.5\n10. Cross-platform path separator note - Yes, in edge cases\n11. `server/utils.ts` structural assumption - Yes, in code references and Decision 1\n12. ESM import specifier convention - Yes, in Conventions section\n13. Vercel install behavior concern and fallback - Yes, Phase 7, Step 7.2\n14. Content uses `content/` not `pages/` - Yes, in docs addendum\n15. Build env variable in vercel.json - Yes, in docs addendum\n\nLet me check one thing I noticed in the Codex log notes -- it mentions the \"Codex Pass Notes\" were appended to the Opus `log.md`. This confirms Codex was built on top of Opus's work. Let me also check if there's something I should add about the `sync-public.yml` workflow -- specifically that it runs `bun private/scripts/sync-public.ts` which means it already runs from a working directory above the private repo checkout, and the strip lists apply from within the private checkout.\n\nActually, looking at sync-public.yml more carefully, I notice one thing: the workflow step at line 84 runs `bun private/scripts/sync-public.ts extract-changelog --version ...`. The `private/` prefix is because the workflow checks out the private repo into a `private/` directory. This means after migration, when the workflow checks out the monorepo at a tag, the `docs/` directory will be present in `private/`. The `STRIP_DIRS` addition of `\"docs\"` to `sync-public.ts` will handle stripping it. This should work as-is -- no workflow file changes needed.\n\nLet me update the merged plan with a couple of small additions I noticed are missing."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1272,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1591,"cache_read_input_tokens":132435,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1591},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"cd6ca204-46d2-496e-91b7-8203c0eaae6d","timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:38:40.251Z"}
