{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013URH2t5MyJyU2wwW2Ngead","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Task: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n     2→\n     3→## Intent\n     4→\n     5→From the task specification in `read_only_data_source/task.md`:\n     6→\n     7→> \"Migrate the Hankweave project from its current multi-repository structure into a unified monorepo. This includes the runtime (`hankweave`), documentation (`hankweave-docs`), and any related packages/tools.\"\n     8→\n     9→The stated goals are:\n    10→\n    11→> - Consolidate all Hankweave repositories into a single monorepo\n    12→> - Maintain clean package boundaries with proper workspace configuration\n    13→> - Preserve git history where practical\n    14→> - Ensure CI/CD pipelines work with the new structure\n    15→> - Keep the developer experience smooth (build, test, lint across packages)\n    16→\n    17→Stated considerations from the task spec:\n    18→\n    19→> - Bun workspaces vs other monorepo tooling (turborepo, nx, etc.)\n    20→> - Package publishing strategy (npm packages from monorepo)\n    21→> - Schema sharing between packages\n    22→> - Test infrastructure consolidation\n    23→> - Documentation site build integration\n    24→> - Binary build pipeline for cross-platform releases\n    25→\n    26→---\n    27→\n    28→## Context\n    29→\n    30→### What exists today\n    31→\n    32→There are currently three repos involved in the Hankweave release pipeline:\n    33→\n    34→1. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`** (private) — the core TypeScript/Bun runtime. Package `@southbridgeai/hankweave`, currently at `v0.5.6`. All active development happens here on a `develop → release/alpha` branch model.\n    35→\n    36→2. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs`** (separate private repo) — the documentation site. Next.js + Nextra 4.x, deployed to Vercel. Package name `hankweave-docs`, version `2.0.0`.\n    37→\n    38→3. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime`** (public) — receives sanitized snapshots from the private runtime repo via a `sync-public` GitHub Actions workflow. Publishes the public `hankweave` npm package accessible as `bunx hankweave` / `npx hankweave`.\n    39→\n    40→### The already-existing cross-repo dependency (key finding)\n    41→\n    42→There is a live implicit dependency between the two dev repos that makes this migration particularly motivated: `hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` (lines 26–31) already reaches into the docs repo:\n    43→\n    44→```typescript\n    45→const DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n    46→  import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"..\",\n    47→  \"hankweave-docs\", \"nextra-site\", \"public\", \"schemas\",\n    48→);\n    49→```\n    50→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"424193f8-5a8a-42b8-9942-76143e044b05","uuid":"52a75d2d-9d45-42c9-9fde-2da246585ccd","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/task.md","content":"# Task: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n\n## Intent\n\nFrom the task specification in `read_only_data_source/task.md`:\n\n> \"Migrate the Hankweave project from its current multi-repository structure into a unified monorepo. This includes the runtime (`hankweave`), documentation (`hankweave-docs`), and any related packages/tools.\"\n\nThe stated goals are:\n\n> - Consolidate all Hankweave repositories into a single monorepo\n> - Maintain clean package boundaries with proper workspace configuration\n> - Preserve git history where practical\n> - Ensure CI/CD pipelines work with the new structure\n> - Keep the developer experience smooth (build, test, lint across packages)\n\nStated considerations from the task spec:\n\n> - Bun workspaces vs other monorepo tooling (turborepo, nx, etc.)\n> - Package publishing strategy (npm packages from monorepo)\n> - Schema sharing between packages\n> - Test infrastructure consolidation\n> - Documentation site build integration\n> - Binary build pipeline for cross-platform releases\n\n---\n\n## Context\n\n### What exists today\n\nThere are currently three repos involved in the Hankweave release pipeline:\n\n1. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`** (private) — the core TypeScript/Bun runtime. Package `@southbridgeai/hankweave`, currently at `v0.5.6`. All active development happens here on a `develop → release/alpha` branch model.\n\n2. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs`** (separate private repo) — the documentation site. Next.js + Nextra 4.x, deployed to Vercel. Package name `hankweave-docs`, version `2.0.0`.\n\n3. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime`** (public) — receives sanitized snapshots from the private runtime repo via a `sync-public` GitHub Actions workflow. Publishes the public `hankweave` npm package accessible as `bunx hankweave` / `npx hankweave`.\n\n### The already-existing cross-repo dependency (key finding)\n\nThere is a live implicit dependency between the two dev repos that makes this migration particularly motivated: `hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` (lines 26–31) already reaches into the docs repo:\n\n```typescript\nconst DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n  import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"..\",\n  \"hankweave-docs\", \"nextra-site\", \"public\", \"schemas\",\n);\n```\n","numLines":50,"startLine":1,"totalLines":214}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:39:12.473Z"}
